9	10	2	processed\\pages\\1780472197296-GraphQL-2.png	📌 Response: { "data": { "user": { "name": "John Doe", "email": "john@example.com", "posts": [ { "title": "GraphQL Basics", "content": "GraphQL is awesome!" } ] } } } Here, the client only requests the name, email, and posts of the user, without unnecessary data. 🔹 GraphQL vs REST: Feature GraphQL REST Data Fetching Fetches only needed data Over-fetches/Under-fetches Endpoints Single endpoint (/graphql) Multiple endpoints Real-time Supports subscriptions Requires WebSockets Versioning No versioning needed Requires versioning (v1, v2) Schema Strongly typed schema Depends on implementation Here's a step-by-step guide to setting up GraphQL in Eclipse with Java Spring Boot :	processed\\qrcodes\\1780472197296-GraphQL.docx-page-2-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-03 00:36:55.36382	5433492e4530555ab13fe1ca3a9bad8eaef546893807b56900ed7f72a597cf53	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780472197296-GraphQL.docx", "pageNumber": 2}
10	10	3	processed\\pages\\1780472197296-GraphQL-3.png	Step 1: Install Eclipse and Setup a Spring Boot Project 1. Download and install Eclipse IDE (if not installed) from: https://www.eclipse.org/downloads/ 2. Install Spring Tools (if not already installed): o Go to Help → Eclipse Marketplace → Search for Spring Tools 4 and install it. 3. Create a Spring Boot Project : o Open Eclipse → File → New → Spring Starter Project o Enter project name (e.g., graphql-demo) o Choose Spring Boot version : 3.x or 2.x o Add dependencies:  Spring Web  Spring Boot DevTools  GraphQL for Spring Boot  Lombok (optional, for reducing boilerplate code) Step 2: Add Dependencies in pom.xml Modify pom.xml to include GraphQL support: <dependencies> <!-- Spring Boot Starter Web --> <dependency> <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId> <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId> </dependency> <!-- Spring Boot Starter GraphQL --> <dependency> <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId> <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-graphql</artifactId> </dependency>	processed\\qrcodes\\1780472197296-GraphQL.docx-page-3-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-03 00:36:55.367951	cea392abf3d27e4ba357ea27600b0ae98268a2dbc9af2ea4b04c97f32c830520	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780472197296-GraphQL.docx", "pageNumber": 3}
11	10	4	processed\\pages\\1780472197296-GraphQL-4.png	<!-- GraphQL Java Tools --> <dependency> <groupId>com.graphql-java-kickstart</groupId> <artifactId>graphql-java-tools</artifactId> <version>11.0.0</version> </dependency> <!-- Lombok (Optional) --> <dependency> <groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId> <artifactId>lombok</artifactId> <scope>provided</scope> </dependency> <!-- H2 Database (For Testing) --> <dependency> <groupId>com.h2database</groupId> <artifactId>h2</artifactId> <scope>runtime</scope> </dependency> </dependencies> Step 3: Configure application.properties Add the following to src/main/resources/application.properties: spring.graphql.graphiql.enabled=true spring.h2.console.enabled=true  This enables the GraphiQL UI for testing GraphQL queries.  Enables H2 database (in-memory database for testing).	processed\\qrcodes\\1780472197296-GraphQL.docx-page-4-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-03 00:36:55.369281	2fda8a161c5be7e29fd65066d6c0dceb349d8ae2389a8e50f975c0d7e5454a1d	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780472197296-GraphQL.docx", "pageNumber": 4}
12	10	5	processed\\pages\\1780472197296-GraphQL-5.png	Step 4: Create a Model Class (Book.java) Create a new package com.example.graphql.model, and inside it, create Book.java: package com.example.graphql.model; import lombok.AllArgsConstructor; import lombok.Data; import lombok.NoArgsConstructor; @Data @AllArgsConstructor @NoArgsConstructor public class Book { private String id; private String title; private String author; private int pages; } Step 5: Create a Repository (BookRepository.java) Create a new package com.example.graphql.repository, and inside it, create BookRepository.java: package com.example.graphql.repository; import com.example.graphql.model.Book; import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository; import java.util.ArrayList; import java.util.List;	processed\\qrcodes\\1780472197296-GraphQL.docx-page-5-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-03 00:36:55.370359	94bd5d892c7da602f3ff7472ec399616b3fdd549c44214e204e3594c6169c925	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780472197296-GraphQL.docx", "pageNumber": 5}
13	10	6	processed\\pages\\1780472197296-GraphQL-6.png	@Repository public class BookRepository { private List<Book> books = new ArrayList<>(); public BookRepository() { books.add(new Book("1", "GraphQL for Java", "John Doe", 200)); books.add(new Book("2", "Spring Boot with GraphQL", "Jane Smith", 300)); } public List<Book> getAllBooks() { return books; } public Book getBookById(String id) { return books.stream() .filter(book -> book.getId().equals(id)) .findFirst() .orElse(null); } } Step 6: Create a GraphQL Query Resolver (BookQueryResolver.java) Create a new package com.example.graphql.resolver, and inside it, create BookQueryResolver.java: package com.example.graphql.resolver; import com.example.graphql.model.Book; import com.example.graphql.repository.BookRepository; import org.springframework.graphql.data.method.annotation.QueryMapping;	processed\\qrcodes\\1780472197296-GraphQL.docx-page-6-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-03 00:36:55.371223	79010f7f62598d1c53d2a02c85a2a5bff712761efba8d1418d646bf5170c886d	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780472197296-GraphQL.docx", "pageNumber": 6}
14	10	7	processed\\pages\\1780472197296-GraphQL-7.png	import org.springframework.stereotype.Controller; import java.util.List; @Controller public class BookQueryResolver { private final BookRepository bookRepository; public BookQueryResolver(BookRepository bookRepository) { this.bookRepository = bookRepository; } @QueryMapping public List<Book> books() { return bookRepository.getAllBooks(); } @QueryMapping public Book book(String id) { return bookRepository.getBookById(id); } } Step 7: Create a GraphQL Schema (schema.graphqls) Create a new file inside src/main/resources/graphql/ named schema.graphqls: type Book { id: ID title: String author: String pages: Int	processed\\qrcodes\\1780472197296-GraphQL.docx-page-7-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-03 00:36:55.372135	32b02b1bdfcfc9469d134e343d6797c727dfbe6bd78219abd355b917c48ee236	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780472197296-GraphQL.docx", "pageNumber": 7}
