18	11	2	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-02.png	3 Origins and Evolution Kanban is a Japanese word that literally means " visual card ". Kanban cards were originally used in Toyota to limit the amount of inventory tied up in " work in progress " on a manufacturing floor. Kanban not only reduces excess inventory waste, but also the time spent in producing it. The original author of Kanban was Taiichi Ohno . Kanban has been used in Lean Production (Lean Thinking) KANBAN was formalized in 2010 by David Anderson	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-2-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.034725	c5826bb5d8388dd95f016be01f4fc25e030be5ac2ef85862ac2a872c3582c737	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 2}
19	11	3	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-03.png	Kanban Concepts	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-3-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.036023	6cdb93d788c822e1a4ce47b0f7a7d2668d4e865cd15830fec38e798c9c5ef41f	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 3}
20	11	4	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-04.png	5 Kanban Concept Cores Visualize Visualize Workflow • Split the entire work into defined sections or states, visualized as named columns on a wall. • Write each item on a card and put in a column to indicate where the item is in the workflow. Limit Limit WIP • Assign explicit limits to how many items can be in progress at each workflow section / state. • i.e., Work in Progress (WIP) is limited in each workflow state. Measure Measure the Lead Time • Lead Time, also known as cycle time, is the average time to complete one item. • Measure the Lead Time and optimize the process to make the Lead Time as small and predictable as possible. Direct implementation of a Lean Pull Scheduling System . An item can move to the next segment / state only when it obtains a slot in there.	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-4-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.037216	c2e17153538fbf4c527d356d75e82d800aab522d8690d7975c8d64ce5b310307	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 4}
21	11	5	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-05.png	6 Kanban Board In a KANBAN process, the practice of visualization consists in making visible the state of the project by using a table and cards customized This is can also be done in a virtual way using computer softwares	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-5-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.038417	82e8d64515edeccec4bba68adfe4eca3f4c09088077b9e23c14d35745dd2b698	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 5}
22	11	6	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-06.png	Kanban Benefits & Characteristics	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-6-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.039668	7ac8c28193abc2078570b1ca710869d1f9bb98a6d4152d463bb505e605d122e3	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 6}
23	11	7	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-07.png	8 Kanban Benefits • It clearly identifies the Bottlenecks in real-time. • It is useful for situations where operations and support teams have a high level of uncertainty and variability . • It increases visibility of everything that is going on at the company. – All segments/states in the workflow are visually organized , – Reducing the wait times and ensuring speed • Reduces inventory in the range of 25%-75%, thereby reducing company costs. • It allows to avoid inventory overproduction, thereby saving resources and time as well. – This is termed as eliminating waste . • In agile, if values are combined with Kanban characteristics, the outcome would be Agile Kanban . – Popular practice in software engineering	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-7-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.041067	fde02f0a0c2e2f3d2bb41c9c9438f708be055d9d52d7c6e1d2815a110343490f	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 7}
24	11	8	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-08.png	9 Kanban Characteristics Flexibility in Planning • Visual representation of the workflow allows creating clearly marked flow lanes and indicating where each item is in the workflow. • If a task needs longer duration, it is allowed to execute without limitation, and at the same time, the tasks that are completed will flow to the next state. – Sufficient duration for longer tasks that cannot be broken down logically. – Preservation of value of such longer tasks. – Effort required by each role to be expended. – Continuous flow of the tasks that are completed without wait time.	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-8-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.042364	48e5b35c51ba00ff17cacf6deb93f245b4e4d31147d4d15b7fd5c0d7489b08ae	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 8}
25	11	9	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-09.png	10 Kanban Characteristics Limits Work-In-Progress (WIP) • Explicit limits of Work-In-Progress (WIP) are assigned to number of items that can be in progress at each workflow state, indicated by a column. • This allows: – Reducing wait time. – Avoiding stress on resources at a workflow state. – Identifying bottlenecks causing an item to be in a workflow state than the anticipated time (usually average cycle time) immediately. – Resolving bottlenecks with collaboration of the entire team. – Decreasing dependencies in completing a task by splitting it into sub-tasks, so that the sub-task is tracked independently.	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-9-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.043434	08fbbd080b7db1bcdd6bd92fb860e7aa6045d8ea643aaa80022dee733a2d9c7a	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 9}
26	11	10	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-10.png	11 Kanban Characteristics Pull Approach • The importance of this approach consists in resolving friction between teams. – i.e., two teams collaborate while the first one is performing better than the second one, – it is likely that it pushes more work than the other can handle. – In Pull Approach, the next team pulls work only when it is ready for it. • Pull Approach is implemented by adding a buffer with limited capacity between the two teams. • The benefits of Pull Approach are: – Avoids piling-up of work. – Reduces wait time. – Facilitates a team to maintain constant pace and focus on quality. – Provides resource balancing.	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-10-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.044345	3593746385d58a9d676f41c083799d5cb7105b3ca11ac3ff575f60a257ffc1e9	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 10}
27	11	11	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-11.png	12 Kanban Characteristics Minimize Cycle Time • The cycle time for each task is measured and the process is optimized to reduce the cycle times. • The bottlenecks are identified immediately and resolved collaboratively by the entire team. • The correction loops are considered to reduce rework.	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-11-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.04694	a6f145c1f7e6ab2d11481ee3c7d4444e461c6986e4b55f9954144f405a1db6bf	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 11}
28	11	12	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-12.png	13 Kanban Characteristics Visual Metrics • Visually organized workflows (on Kanban Boards) facilitate: – Scheduling as per WIP limits on a workflow state. – Tracking status and progress continually. – Assigning resources dynamically based on the role requirements.	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-12-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.047983	53d3d91a0a74cd9eefcb4660775264c39256f44bd7967a7c37187fcdc1e2045a	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 12}
29	11	13	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-13.png	14 Kanban Characteristics Continuous Delivery Benefits of continuous delivery are: • Short release cycles result in continuous delivery of growing product at regular interval. • Continuous interactions with the customer. • To understand what customer wants. • Produce what the customer needs. • Feedback on delivered modules. • Limited requirements in each release cycle. • Developers are not overloaded with requests. This enables them to focus on the delivery. • There is no partially completed work. • Focus is on finishing work than on starting work. • This enables focus on sustaining pace and quality of the product. • Deliver before the customer changes mind. • Optimize flow of Work from beginning to end. • Helps in incremental process improvements.	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-13-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.049089	18b02a94d68779aeb1bb63791e31318c53b370c6016c651db1c02e2aa1d3e07b	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 13}
30	11	14	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-14.png	15 Kanban Characteristics Efficiency Through Focus The focus is on delivering value to the customer. • A customer's expectations can be made realistic and focused with continuous interactions with the customer. • Focus on the tasks is ensured with a limit on work-in-progress (WIP). • The Pull approach enables resources to complete the tasks at hand before a new task is taken up. • Optimizing lead-time (cycle time) results in faster delivery. • Visualization of the workflow with Kanban board draws immediate attention to any bottlenecks that can be resolved immediately. • Empowerment of the team makes the team accountable for the success. Efficiency can be achieved in the following ways:	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-14-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.050203	d9d885a0b70c8c6ce7e8f82ccf824770f30a081b6573c31fa38d41800070ff14	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 14}
31	11	15	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-15.png	Kanban Practices	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-15-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.053025	d9a25c0a09025c1826f05c2c11bc14ffec31c42d1676cdd2c6c84a7252cc8181	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 15}
32	11	16	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-16.png	17 Kanban Practices A KANBAN process encompasses in general 6 types of practices: Visualization Limitation of Work in Progress (WIP) Control by measure Improvement Detailed explanation of rules Adopting PDSA model	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-16-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.054274	a1eb15f561a73b7099c255ff65ff23fef94501086e108877d1688191e76b3829	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 16}
33	11	17	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-17.png	18 Vizualization In KANBAN process, the visualization practice consists in making visible the state of the project by using a board and customized cards Visualization can also be performed in a virtual way using computer software (ie., Jira)	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-17-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.055323	fc288ba4984ab5c314bd8675ddc654f92cc449b4f7cb2028cefa5afbce32e3a5	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 17}
34	11	18	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-18.png	19 WIP Limitation • WIP (Work In Progress) is streamlined thanks to a limitation of tasks and elements in progress for each activity of the process • Once established , this limit should not be exceeded by the team • The definition of the done must be well defined and understood in the same way by all the team members as well as by stakeholders	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-18-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.056224	265976d87413cf43f1c11f5daf15ea942659f105e447cda01478d4ab972e5056	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 18}
35	11	19	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-19.png	20 Control and measure • The control of the elements movement is managed by indicators and measures allowing to anticipate the blockages cases and the possible prioritizations • The daily meetings address and organize the workflow • One example of measurement is that of the cycle time, it is the measuring of the duration (time) necessary to carry out a task or a element	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-19-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.057248	af32a2db05ab650e2fd2f4eac04b843b2d30fe0958a5a2e164bd0c388fbdb14f	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 19}
36	11	20	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-20.png	21 Improvement The blockages and problems encountered are the trigger for a collaborative dialogue within the team in order to: • clearly specify the improvements to be made during an iteration , • and to determine the changes to be made.	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-20-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.058576	b79be532f146a1e25ac2dd64481c8cfbacba071330ee83b4321bdd846f2646c0	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 20}
37	11	21	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-21.png	22 Kanban Rules Explanation • The KANBAN process rules must be explicit and visible to team members to: – ensure a shared understanding of the work , problems encountered and improvements future • One example of a rule consists in defining the common notion of the " Definition of Done " by all the team members .	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-21-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.060062	452879933c9796cbeee0c27eddf2dc82d95bef89cc3d4f22db392ae876d3ceb7	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 21}
38	11	22	processed\\pages\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework-22.png	23 PDSA Model The KANBAN approach is taken from the model PDSA : The phase Plan (planning) consists of identifying a design model and defining the actions to be lead The phase Do (execute) represents the realization and implementation The phase Study (study) is the analysis and study phase The phase Act (acting) is the phase of adjusting and consolidating the model	processed\\qrcodes\\1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-22-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-04 00:43:11.061221	bcdd56bea7f49567090ae484db67febd663ada301ea931e8cdd1aa5a9087d512	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1780558974331-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 22}
