45	17	2	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-02.png	3 Origins and Evolution Kanban is a Japanese word that literally means " visual card ". Kanban cards were originally used in Toyota to limit the amount of inventory tied up in " work in progress " on a manufacturing floor. Kanban not only reduces excess inventory waste, but also the time spent in producing it. The original author of Kanban was Taiichi Ohno . Kanban has been used in Lean Production (Lean Thinking) KANBAN was formalized in 2010 by David Anderson	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-2-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.602366	1ae4c21c2e57bb13c0d29f2190fc98083f66d6d4a194e09164b83be972016426	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 2}
46	17	3	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-03.png	Kanban Concepts	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-3-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.604012	0155586da6e26d074cb1a2a81d4983f7f684e57e816fce187462791dd39afa1f	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 3}
47	17	4	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-04.png	5 Kanban Concept Cores Visualize Visualize Workflow • Split the entire work into defined sections or states, visualized as named columns on a wall. • Write each item on a card and put in a column to indicate where the item is in the workflow. Limit Limit WIP • Assign explicit limits to how many items can be in progress at each workflow section / state. • i.e., Work in Progress (WIP) is limited in each workflow state. Measure Measure the Lead Time • Lead Time, also known as cycle time, is the average time to complete one item. • Measure the Lead Time and optimize the process to make the Lead Time as small and predictable as possible. Direct implementation of a Lean Pull Scheduling System . An item can move to the next segment / state only when it obtains a slot in there.	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-4-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.605685	c5223cfd1b639271896985ac5cddc29c5b44c9843b69090589fb8ffce888c429	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 4}
48	17	5	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-05.png	6 Kanban Board In a KANBAN process, the practice of visualization consists in making visible the state of the project by using a table and cards customized This is can also be done in a virtual way using computer softwares	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-5-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.607175	3406d29b27c680003f666addab3c06adc579bc46cdbb656f2be0457790148743	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 5}
49	17	6	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-06.png	Kanban Benefits & Characteristics	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-6-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.609659	8b559d5badff6a17c506e0fc383969c818b4cfae6faa6808f410e7f3d107fd7a	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 6}
50	17	7	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-07.png	8 Kanban Benefits • It clearly identifies the Bottlenecks in real-time. • It is useful for situations where operations and support teams have a high level of uncertainty and variability . • It increases visibility of everything that is going on at the company. – All segments/states in the workflow are visually organized , – Reducing the wait times and ensuring speed • Reduces inventory in the range of 25%-75%, thereby reducing company costs. • It allows to avoid inventory overproduction, thereby saving resources and time as well. – This is termed as eliminating waste . • In agile, if values are combined with Kanban characteristics, the outcome would be Agile Kanban . – Popular practice in software engineering	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-7-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.611475	2483da86e085773ef1e998c57483599e66e2bf2b5c7b2ec1f02a7a3d9e9c7469	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 7}
51	17	8	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-08.png	9 Kanban Characteristics Flexibility in Planning • Visual representation of the workflow allows creating clearly marked flow lanes and indicating where each item is in the workflow. • If a task needs longer duration, it is allowed to execute without limitation, and at the same time, the tasks that are completed will flow to the next state. – Sufficient duration for longer tasks that cannot be broken down logically. – Preservation of value of such longer tasks. – Effort required by each role to be expended. – Continuous flow of the tasks that are completed without wait time.	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-8-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.61282	9deb9dd794b8c0dba5f4de3fbf3530ca576b5158d0041019b7d1874983800466	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 8}
52	17	9	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-09.png	10 Kanban Characteristics Limits Work-In-Progress (WIP) • Explicit limits of Work-In-Progress (WIP) are assigned to number of items that can be in progress at each workflow state, indicated by a column. • This allows: – Reducing wait time. – Avoiding stress on resources at a workflow state. – Identifying bottlenecks causing an item to be in a workflow state than the anticipated time (usually average cycle time) immediately. – Resolving bottlenecks with collaboration of the entire team. – Decreasing dependencies in completing a task by splitting it into sub-tasks, so that the sub-task is tracked independently.	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-9-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.61423	fe351729ef39da731721062fa19ea4623f87be2b85f56ceedc690da66f96df4f	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 9}
53	17	10	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-10.png	11 Kanban Characteristics Pull Approach • The importance of this approach consists in resolving friction between teams. – i.e., two teams collaborate while the first one is performing better than the second one, – it is likely that it pushes more work than the other can handle. – In Pull Approach, the next team pulls work only when it is ready for it. • Pull Approach is implemented by adding a buffer with limited capacity between the two teams. • The benefits of Pull Approach are: – Avoids piling-up of work. – Reduces wait time. – Facilitates a team to maintain constant pace and focus on quality. – Provides resource balancing.	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-10-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.615792	1cdf60406a1729b32a2cd83afdd85fe99e552aa9494a1fe3b82165ea523b7c27	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 10}
54	17	11	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-11.png	12 Kanban Characteristics Minimize Cycle Time • The cycle time for each task is measured and the process is optimized to reduce the cycle times. • The bottlenecks are identified immediately and resolved collaboratively by the entire team. • The correction loops are considered to reduce rework.	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-11-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.617925	7d056919265cef0d134471f6235b67b87f5c88932e2d286d7a1467a5e4a28975	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 11}
55	17	12	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-12.png	13 Kanban Characteristics Visual Metrics • Visually organized workflows (on Kanban Boards) facilitate: – Scheduling as per WIP limits on a workflow state. – Tracking status and progress continually. – Assigning resources dynamically based on the role requirements.	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-12-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.619445	bfb4dba7d9bbd3f07d00f9322fc888a26373c7196e68aa4976828f9e86da9f37	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 12}
56	17	13	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-13.png	14 Kanban Characteristics Continuous Delivery Benefits of continuous delivery are: • Short release cycles result in continuous delivery of growing product at regular interval. • Continuous interactions with the customer. • To understand what customer wants. • Produce what the customer needs. • Feedback on delivered modules. • Limited requirements in each release cycle. • Developers are not overloaded with requests. This enables them to focus on the delivery. • There is no partially completed work. • Focus is on finishing work than on starting work. • This enables focus on sustaining pace and quality of the product. • Deliver before the customer changes mind. • Optimize flow of Work from beginning to end. • Helps in incremental process improvements.	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-13-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.620893	0fbce5201992d900236a3fab258d9375d28bb035ca82c8b3df5198ab747a02b9	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 13}
57	17	14	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-14.png	15 Kanban Characteristics Efficiency Through Focus The focus is on delivering value to the customer. • A customer's expectations can be made realistic and focused with continuous interactions with the customer. • Focus on the tasks is ensured with a limit on work-in-progress (WIP). • The Pull approach enables resources to complete the tasks at hand before a new task is taken up. • Optimizing lead-time (cycle time) results in faster delivery. • Visualization of the workflow with Kanban board draws immediate attention to any bottlenecks that can be resolved immediately. • Empowerment of the team makes the team accountable for the success. Efficiency can be achieved in the following ways:	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-14-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.62242	f19a02bfb5a3e004d249f3b5a30c604b1e756c81ff2d21d8244e2eebbd6632cb	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 14}
58	17	15	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-15.png	Kanban Practices	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-15-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.624428	2b46df0e7321386a2ce9ce8f3af8d84005b545043c9c37773c1b83cf757f748e	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 15}
59	17	16	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-16.png	17 Kanban Practices A KANBAN process encompasses in general 6 types of practices: Visualization Limitation of Work in Progress (WIP) Control by measure Improvement Detailed explanation of rules Adopting PDSA model	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-16-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.626321	bb0fb0f1750cb611e7e9f245c07ca3d127f11bc61cf055c909ad68c32a3ae57e	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 16}
60	17	17	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-17.png	18 Vizualization In KANBAN process, the visualization practice consists in making visible the state of the project by using a board and customized cards Visualization can also be performed in a virtual way using computer software (ie., Jira)	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-17-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.627772	083f612a3181cf802548dd876177aaa2b4fcf485c1f3439e914815da853b8e75	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 17}
61	17	18	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-18.png	19 WIP Limitation • WIP (Work In Progress) is streamlined thanks to a limitation of tasks and elements in progress for each activity of the process • Once established , this limit should not be exceeded by the team • The definition of the done must be well defined and understood in the same way by all the team members as well as by stakeholders	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-18-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.629378	2e0e077712deeb006f9fa0af6f15003eb1123fa8f52cc33443732a73820eff1b	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 18}
62	17	19	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-19.png	20 Control and measure • The control of the elements movement is managed by indicators and measures allowing to anticipate the blockages cases and the possible prioritizations • The daily meetings address and organize the workflow • One example of measurement is that of the cycle time, it is the measuring of the duration (time) necessary to carry out a task or a element	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-19-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.630933	ad351f69e0330c5f1454bc1da69fab59fa483adc47d73d9f3c30579888d91483	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 19}
63	17	20	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-20.png	21 Improvement The blockages and problems encountered are the trigger for a collaborative dialogue within the team in order to: • clearly specify the improvements to be made during an iteration , • and to determine the changes to be made.	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-20-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.632574	2c5c4b099c3f82c73afdd52133fde726f9721c03e62a4a6248aecd19d835a26b	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 20}
64	17	21	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-21.png	22 Kanban Rules Explanation • The KANBAN process rules must be explicit and visible to team members to: – ensure a shared understanding of the work , problems encountered and improvements future • One example of a rule consists in defining the common notion of the " Definition of Done " by all the team members .	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-21-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.634766	c2313add47134529e61bbf941004dcfbe21b02498de6ef044da107c66466f799	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 21}
65	17	22	processed\\pages\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework-22.png	23 PDSA Model The KANBAN approach is taken from the model PDSA : The phase Plan (planning) consists of identifying a design model and defining the actions to be lead The phase Do (execute) represents the realization and implementation The phase Study (study) is the analysis and study phase The phase Act (acting) is the phase of adjusting and consolidating the model	processed\\qrcodes\\1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-22-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-15 07:32:07.636433	1c83228153115053cdf0416f9276980c764212d9d4e63d0aabf71a8293d2112f	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781533909251-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 22}
