78	18	13	processed\\pages\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework-13.png	14 Kanban Characteristics Continuous Delivery Benefits of continuous delivery are: • Short release cycles result in continuous delivery of growing product at regular interval. • Continuous interactions with the customer. • To understand what customer wants. • Produce what the customer needs. • Feedback on delivered modules. • Limited requirements in each release cycle. • Developers are not overloaded with requests. This enables them to focus on the delivery. • There is no partially completed work. • Focus is on finishing work than on starting work. • This enables focus on sustaining pace and quality of the product. • Deliver before the customer changes mind. • Optimize flow of Work from beginning to end. • Helps in incremental process improvements.	processed\\qrcodes\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-13-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-16 06:57:12.282915	fad4f324416e4b5e8a733c45461216036913b31383f801ca4a267d9a3c8ed9f5	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 13}
79	18	14	processed\\pages\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework-14.png	15 Kanban Characteristics Efficiency Through Focus The focus is on delivering value to the customer. • A customer's expectations can be made realistic and focused with continuous interactions with the customer. • Focus on the tasks is ensured with a limit on work-in-progress (WIP). • The Pull approach enables resources to complete the tasks at hand before a new task is taken up. • Optimizing lead-time (cycle time) results in faster delivery. • Visualization of the workflow with Kanban board draws immediate attention to any bottlenecks that can be resolved immediately. • Empowerment of the team makes the team accountable for the success. Efficiency can be achieved in the following ways:	processed\\qrcodes\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-14-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-16 06:57:12.284733	bafa911af07d45de9bd1bd72841d3f4ce08a82ede4556d14e51b6fef094d2375	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 14}
80	18	15	processed\\pages\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework-15.png	Kanban Practices	processed\\qrcodes\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-15-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-16 06:57:12.286222	7a101cb718ac6e443550e6222eda7e254c8780d53be88c612f5ea4d52b94d2d0	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 15}
81	18	16	processed\\pages\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework-16.png	17 Kanban Practices A KANBAN process encompasses in general 6 types of practices: Visualization Limitation of Work in Progress (WIP) Control by measure Improvement Detailed explanation of rules Adopting PDSA model	processed\\qrcodes\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-16-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-16 06:57:12.287548	cb53ef1fa54df76187b92269e8664e1d25d58483af59a09bb69cfbf85acc65bc	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 16}
82	18	17	processed\\pages\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework-17.png	18 Vizualization In KANBAN process, the visualization practice consists in making visible the state of the project by using a board and customized cards Visualization can also be performed in a virtual way using computer software (ie., Jira)	processed\\qrcodes\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-17-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-16 06:57:12.288998	10fc50d30f6e468373417faf4061c0776c698a77b3c85ee2b5713e5e9d4dc444	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 17}
83	18	18	processed\\pages\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework-18.png	19 WIP Limitation • WIP (Work In Progress) is streamlined thanks to a limitation of tasks and elements in progress for each activity of the process • Once established , this limit should not be exceeded by the team • The definition of the done must be well defined and understood in the same way by all the team members as well as by stakeholders	processed\\qrcodes\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-18-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-16 06:57:12.290823	16077b6fbaafae250cbfa1bf32f3d283690585589a8a0cbbfed586327de068f2	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 18}
84	18	19	processed\\pages\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework-19.png	20 Control and measure • The control of the elements movement is managed by indicators and measures allowing to anticipate the blockages cases and the possible prioritizations • The daily meetings address and organize the workflow • One example of measurement is that of the cycle time, it is the measuring of the duration (time) necessary to carry out a task or a element	processed\\qrcodes\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-19-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-16 06:57:12.292353	5dc901d2b7e092982267fe4fc77c712f42996f67a27cb02b5000c6c2f49dd432	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 19}
85	18	20	processed\\pages\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework-20.png	21 Improvement The blockages and problems encountered are the trigger for a collaborative dialogue within the team in order to: • clearly specify the improvements to be made during an iteration , • and to determine the changes to be made.	processed\\qrcodes\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-20-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-16 06:57:12.293794	b070b9f2008b05a10a832a5fff8c098751db9f459aa7a5f58c3f175b459abf8b	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 20}
86	18	21	processed\\pages\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework-21.png	22 Kanban Rules Explanation • The KANBAN process rules must be explicit and visible to team members to: – ensure a shared understanding of the work , problems encountered and improvements future • One example of a rule consists in defining the common notion of the " Definition of Done " by all the team members .	processed\\qrcodes\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-21-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-16 06:57:12.295397	e94f86402864e75009dc21ddda4a6c70f4fadd909d72183edbc2c7f30cd1078b	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 21}
87	18	22	processed\\pages\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework-22.png	23 PDSA Model The KANBAN approach is taken from the model PDSA : The phase Plan (planning) consists of identifying a design model and defining the actions to be lead The phase Do (execute) represents the realization and implementation The phase Study (study) is the analysis and study phase The phase Act (acting) is the phase of adjusting and consolidating the model	processed\\qrcodes\\1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-22-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-16 06:57:12.296877	e8a7a171f206139245b81f4ed250f1e0ef68dda29c0fc8e4eb29eca8e3ec0fa4	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1781618211876-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 22}
