103	26	4	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-04.png	5 Kanban Concept Cores Visualize Visualize Workflow • Split the entire work into defined sections or states, visualized as named columns on a wall. • Write each item on a card and put in a column to indicate where the item is in the workflow. Limit Limit WIP • Assign explicit limits to how many items can be in progress at each workflow section / state. • i.e., Work in Progress (WIP) is limited in each workflow state. Measure Measure the Lead Time • Lead Time, also known as cycle time, is the average time to complete one item. • Measure the Lead Time and optimize the process to make the Lead Time as small and predictable as possible. Direct implementation of a Lean Pull Scheduling System . An item can move to the next segment / state only when it obtains a slot in there.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-4-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.125634	7ca68116763b92185eaa503f7e53814579d5e60a3f7c60f4411dc5e6455c2918	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 4}
104	26	5	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-05.png	6 Kanban Board In a KANBAN process, the practice of visualization consists in making visible the state of the project by using a table and cards customized This is can also be done in a virtual way using computer softwares	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-5-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.127706	8d42e5f568dcabdd98dcc4358608db8919d2619be0e318b17a53abcb2e5e5fbe	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 5}
105	26	6	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-06.png	Kanban Benefits & Characteristics	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-6-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.129612	c4254a4dd4ccc605ab5b2f645e5c9b3bb1f42fdfdb14ea441a29eea2e22e1c62	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 6}
106	26	7	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-07.png	8 Kanban Benefits • It clearly identifies the Bottlenecks in real-time. • It is useful for situations where operations and support teams have a high level of uncertainty and variability . • It increases visibility of everything that is going on at the company. – All segments/states in the workflow are visually organized , – Reducing the wait times and ensuring speed • Reduces inventory in the range of 25%-75%, thereby reducing company costs. • It allows to avoid inventory overproduction, thereby saving resources and time as well. – This is termed as eliminating waste . • In agile, if values are combined with Kanban characteristics, the outcome would be Agile Kanban . – Popular practice in software engineering	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-7-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.130963	df11094ebb3fe71108757e043d2082412a68b943bb2ef2295dfa03c81dcbc877	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 7}
107	26	8	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-08.png	9 Kanban Characteristics Flexibility in Planning • Visual representation of the workflow allows creating clearly marked flow lanes and indicating where each item is in the workflow. • If a task needs longer duration, it is allowed to execute without limitation, and at the same time, the tasks that are completed will flow to the next state. – Sufficient duration for longer tasks that cannot be broken down logically. – Preservation of value of such longer tasks. – Effort required by each role to be expended. – Continuous flow of the tasks that are completed without wait time.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-8-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.132089	bdc196c3635efee485e46fb477264f9fd72ff0e25808f42eccc6681e90625949	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 8}
108	26	9	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-09.png	10 Kanban Characteristics Limits Work-In-Progress (WIP) • Explicit limits of Work-In-Progress (WIP) are assigned to number of items that can be in progress at each workflow state, indicated by a column. • This allows: – Reducing wait time. – Avoiding stress on resources at a workflow state. – Identifying bottlenecks causing an item to be in a workflow state than the anticipated time (usually average cycle time) immediately. – Resolving bottlenecks with collaboration of the entire team. – Decreasing dependencies in completing a task by splitting it into sub-tasks, so that the sub-task is tracked independently.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-9-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.133024	aa18e59dac3b049573f8df1804721ddf00905a89c6e2ff24fdc8dc55a3611b56	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 9}
109	26	10	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-10.png	11 Kanban Characteristics Pull Approach • The importance of this approach consists in resolving friction between teams. – i.e., two teams collaborate while the first one is performing better than the second one, – it is likely that it pushes more work than the other can handle. – In Pull Approach, the next team pulls work only when it is ready for it. • Pull Approach is implemented by adding a buffer with limited capacity between the two teams. • The benefits of Pull Approach are: – Avoids piling-up of work. – Reduces wait time. – Facilitates a team to maintain constant pace and focus on quality. – Provides resource balancing.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-10-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.13394	c2d6df0a0b498cf52120d68aeaeb426ba7adb0fd996c9cc2e3b83a3313f147ca	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 10}
110	26	11	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-11.png	12 Kanban Characteristics Minimize Cycle Time • The cycle time for each task is measured and the process is optimized to reduce the cycle times. • The bottlenecks are identified immediately and resolved collaboratively by the entire team. • The correction loops are considered to reduce rework.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-11-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.135757	06721344925059ecb6b1d3ce0f998dc3063041baab2e850b3b04fd407ef0368b	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 11}
111	26	12	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-12.png	13 Kanban Characteristics Visual Metrics • Visually organized workflows (on Kanban Boards) facilitate: – Scheduling as per WIP limits on a workflow state. – Tracking status and progress continually. – Assigning resources dynamically based on the role requirements.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-12-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.136816	57796e85c648beb18a825d61097c22db36655e8bb6fc4a1278152d417fa4f95b	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 12}
112	26	13	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-13.png	14 Kanban Characteristics Continuous Delivery Benefits of continuous delivery are: • Short release cycles result in continuous delivery of growing product at regular interval. • Continuous interactions with the customer. • To understand what customer wants. • Produce what the customer needs. • Feedback on delivered modules. • Limited requirements in each release cycle. • Developers are not overloaded with requests. This enables them to focus on the delivery. • There is no partially completed work. • Focus is on finishing work than on starting work. • This enables focus on sustaining pace and quality of the product. • Deliver before the customer changes mind. • Optimize flow of Work from beginning to end. • Helps in incremental process improvements.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-13-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.137742	151059d18c7dc96956fd860235527ac37842af06c763ee761e06c33f851def64	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 13}
113	26	14	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-14.png	15 Kanban Characteristics Efficiency Through Focus The focus is on delivering value to the customer. • A customer's expectations can be made realistic and focused with continuous interactions with the customer. • Focus on the tasks is ensured with a limit on work-in-progress (WIP). • The Pull approach enables resources to complete the tasks at hand before a new task is taken up. • Optimizing lead-time (cycle time) results in faster delivery. • Visualization of the workflow with Kanban board draws immediate attention to any bottlenecks that can be resolved immediately. • Empowerment of the team makes the team accountable for the success. Efficiency can be achieved in the following ways:	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-14-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.13892	4f8a8cb4f20f3f122592b1544f694dd053f716f599c332d232ea7d0a2f3405a4	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 14}
114	26	15	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-15.png	Kanban Practices	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-15-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.139854	7be27bec0ecf1d9ac229c05a65ae63cf04e9a60b50e915670c3b6e66a51f88bd	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 15}
115	26	16	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-16.png	17 Kanban Practices A KANBAN process encompasses in general 6 types of practices: Visualization Limitation of Work in Progress (WIP) Control by measure Improvement Detailed explanation of rules Adopting PDSA model	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-16-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.140778	5f176ad32d99ecc36b8c4042d8ecdb17b00718d7465f30df32232690d8023d10	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 16}
116	26	17	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-17.png	18 Vizualization In KANBAN process, the visualization practice consists in making visible the state of the project by using a board and customized cards Visualization can also be performed in a virtual way using computer software (ie., Jira)	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-17-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.14214	7ae651ac00807b45fdb1bda1d8f5eef1b6b0ebbef941d93e3d0bbe6db40386d9	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 17}
117	26	18	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-18.png	19 WIP Limitation • WIP (Work In Progress) is streamlined thanks to a limitation of tasks and elements in progress for each activity of the process • Once established , this limit should not be exceeded by the team • The definition of the done must be well defined and understood in the same way by all the team members as well as by stakeholders	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-18-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.143355	a3bfe97c51c72d65fffeac497161d4f20e29439a612440dbd45416b707d586b9	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 18}
118	26	19	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-19.png	20 Control and measure • The control of the elements movement is managed by indicators and measures allowing to anticipate the blockages cases and the possible prioritizations • The daily meetings address and organize the workflow • One example of measurement is that of the cycle time, it is the measuring of the duration (time) necessary to carry out a task or a element	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-19-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.14437	229fce5ba01195bba4edb3bfd741a728e32d3c00decd0304f56365e5cd6e99f6	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 19}
119	26	20	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-20.png	21 Improvement The blockages and problems encountered are the trigger for a collaborative dialogue within the team in order to: • clearly specify the improvements to be made during an iteration , • and to determine the changes to be made.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-20-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.146304	64ee9f414d9774a4062d7fbf16ad92378c7c13d73dd7e1d9c50457e5302eb047	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 20}
120	26	21	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-21.png	22 Kanban Rules Explanation • The KANBAN process rules must be explicit and visible to team members to: – ensure a shared understanding of the work , problems encountered and improvements future • One example of a rule consists in defining the common notion of the " Definition of Done " by all the team members .	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-21-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.147595	c7012c582d380fd7d288cc1eb922a864a4e4ab6ff2b5f5097f7421401cabdc99	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 21}
121	26	22	processed\\pages\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework-22.png	23 PDSA Model The KANBAN approach is taken from the model PDSA : The phase Plan (planning) consists of identifying a design model and defining the actions to be lead The phase Do (execute) represents the realization and implementation The phase Study (study) is the analysis and study phase The phase Act (acting) is the phase of adjusting and consolidating the model	processed\\qrcodes\\1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-22-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 11:01:12.148786	2bb120aa62713541c8af0d0652a5c9ee95f8032f5b57b2511d58de06d85fc9c9	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782324055497-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 22}
122	27	1	processed\\pages\\1782331508559-EmployeeManagement (1)-01.png	Exercise: Employee Management Web Service using Apache CXF Objective: Students will create a SOAP-based web service using Apache CXF in Eclipse , which allows clients to: 1. Add a new employee (name, ID, department). 2. Retrieve an employee by ID . 3. Get a list of all employees . They will also build a SOAP client to interact with the web service. Prerequisites: Before starting, ensure the following are installed: • Eclipse IDE (preferably Eclipse IDE for Enterprise Java Developers). • Apache Tomcat (or another servlet container). • JDK 8+ . • Apache CXF libraries (or add as a Maven dependency). Step 1: Create a Maven Web Project in Eclipse 1. Open Eclipse → File → N ew → Maven Project . 2. Select war as the packaging type. 3. Set groupId as com.example and artifactId as EmployeeService. 4. Add the following dependencies in pom.xml : <dependencies> <!-- Apache CXF --> <dependency> <groupId>org.apache.cxf</groupId> <artifactId>cxf-rt-frontend-jaxws</artifactId> <version>3.5.3</version>	processed\\qrcodes\\1782331508559-EmployeeManagement (1).pdf-page-1-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 13:05:19.184771	b683ef8a58c30a621522f3069f4f8cbab6791c174d92cef6fed8d609cebd5b30	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782331508559-EmployeeManagement (1).pdf", "pageNumber": 1}
123	27	2	processed\\pages\\1782331508559-EmployeeManagement (1)-02.png	</dependency> <dependency> <groupId>org.apache.cxf</groupId> <artifactId>cxf-rt-transports-http</artifactId> <version>3.5.3</version> </dependency> <!-- Java EE API --> <dependency> <groupId>javax.xml.ws</groupId> <artifactId>jaxws-api</artifactId> <version>2.3.1</version> </dependency> </dependencies> Step 2: Create the Web Service Interface 1. In src/main/java/com/example/service, create EmployeeService.java : package com.example.service; import javax.jws.WebMethod; import javax.jws.WebService; import java.util.List; @WebService public interface EmployeeService { @WebMethod String addEmployee(String id, String name, String department); @WebMethod String getEmployee(String id); @WebMethod List<String> getAllEmployees();	processed\\qrcodes\\1782331508559-EmployeeManagement (1).pdf-page-2-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 13:05:19.206283	ca82ddc6b3c5b8575112d27f03aab1bfda1e8e532cdd9b6c226718649046c1e0	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782331508559-EmployeeManagement (1).pdf", "pageNumber": 2}
124	27	3	processed\\pages\\1782331508559-EmployeeManagement (1)-03.png	} Step 3: Implement the Web Service 1. Create EmployeeServiceImpl.java in the same package: package com.example.service; import javax.jws.WebService; import java.util.ArrayList; import java.util.HashMap; import java.util.List; import java.util.Map; @WebService(endpointInterface = "com.example.service.EmployeeService") public class EmployeeServiceImpl implements EmployeeService { private static Map<String, String> employees = new HashMap<>(); @Override public String addEmployee(String id, String name, String department) { employees.put(id, name + " - " + department); return "Employee added: " + id; } @Override public String getEmployee(String id) { return employees.getOrDefault(id, "Employee not found"); } @Override public List<String> getAllEmployees() {	processed\\qrcodes\\1782331508559-EmployeeManagement (1).pdf-page-3-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 13:05:19.207776	e5746b1954c79087da749b3de7421a2900d1eff297ca653e18c742bec77c23e8	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782331508559-EmployeeManagement (1).pdf", "pageNumber": 3}
