131	29	1	processed\\pages\\1782331744318-lab3_4_01b_cli_pull_I-1.png	Lab: (Command Line) Pull Requests I Estimated time: 10 minutes Note: This lab assumes that you are using the command line. If you would prefer to use Sourcetree, there are separate instructions. In this lab, you will: 1. Prepare for a single repository pull request. 2. Create a pull request. 3. Merge a pull request 1: Prepare for a single repository pull request. 1. Log into Bitbucket and create a remote repository named projecti . You can have Bitbucket create the first commit here if you would like. 2. If you didn't create commit above, create and commit a README.md file containing the text # PROJECTI README # with a commit message of "initial commit". 3. Clone your repository locally. 4. Using the command line, create a branch named feature1 off of the master branch. This will be the branch that is part of the single repository pull request. 5. Create a commit on the feature1 branch containing a file named fileA.txt with the line "feature 1" as the content of the file. 6. Push the feature1 branch to the remote projecti repository. Congratulations, you have prepared for a single repository pull request. 2: Create a pull request. 1. In Bitbucket, navigate to the projecti repository. Click on Pull requests . Click on Create pull request . 2. In the Create a pull request window, select the feature1 branch on the left and the master branch on the right. Modify the information if you would like and select Create pull request . You should see that your pull request was created. Congratulations, you have created a single repository pull request. 3: Merge a pull request. 1. (If necessary) In Bitbucket, navigate to the projecti repository. Click on Pull requests . You should see your pull request. Click on the link to view the pull request. 2. Click the Merge button associated with your pull request. Accept the default commit message and merge strategy (merge commit). Click Merge . You should see that the feature1 branch is now merged. Click on Commits and verify that the work of feature 1 is now in your commit graph. 3. In the command line, execute a pull to add the new merge commit to your local repository. 4. Delete the feature1 branch label locally and on origin . To delete the origin (remote) branch, view the project in Bitbucket. Select Branches , then All branches . Click the ... next to the branch	processed\\qrcodes\\1782331744318-lab3_4_01b_cli_pull_I.pdf-page-1-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 13:09:08.612357	c8e8c72ea449c88391903631657e85d0b8d4475c1748b1dff0954386b5c13611	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782331744318-lab3_4_01b_cli_pull_I.pdf", "pageNumber": 1}
132	29	2	processed\\pages\\1782331744318-lab3_4_01b_cli_pull_I-2.png	name, then Delete . 5. You will not use the projecti repository in future labs. You can delete it. Congratulations, you have merged a pull request and completed this lab. Copyright © 2020 Atlassian	processed\\qrcodes\\1782331744318-lab3_4_01b_cli_pull_I.pdf-page-2-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 13:09:08.618314	6c62cb51301889deec1fb7cfe2c6fdfeaf098ccebe87677b1c4748a331e4c16d	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782331744318-lab3_4_01b_cli_pull_I.pdf", "pageNumber": 2}
133	30	1	processed\\pages\\1782333391691-DevOps (1)-1.png	DevOps 1⃣ What is DevOps ? DevOps is a culture, not a tool. It improves collaboration between Dev and Ops for faster and better software delivery. 2⃣ DevOps Foundation Principles – The Three Ways of DevOps 1. Flow 2. Feedback 3. Continual Learning 3⃣ First Way – FLOW Goal: Accelerate the flow of work from Development → Operations → Customers. Key Principles of Flow: • Understand the flow of work • Increase flow continuously • Never pass known defects downstream • Avoid local optimization that harms the whole system Example: Fix a bug before moving code to the next stage. 4⃣ Continuous Integration (CI) CI: Developers integrate code frequently with automated tests. 5⃣ Continuous Delivery Software is always ready to be released. Deployment is usually manual 📌 Example: You can deploy anytime, even if you don't deploy every change. 6⃣ Continuous Deployment	processed\\qrcodes\\1782333391691-DevOps (1).pdf-page-1-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 13:36:37.65561	ac6438c61263869386c9134a459b0ca7b51911a11fb35f0af58bd7d3f1a55303	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782333391691-DevOps (1).pdf", "pageNumber": 1}
134	30	2	processed\\pages\\1782333391691-DevOps (1)-2.png	Every change that passes tests is deployed automatically. Difference from Continuous Delivery: • Delivery → Manual deploy • Deployment → Automatic deploy Note: Not suitable for all companies (regulations). Release vs Deployment • Deployment: Install software • Release: Make features visible to users Example: Code is deployed but feature is hidden. First Way in Action • Visualize work using Kanban (JIRA) • Limit Work In Progress (WIP) • Use automation tools Second Way – FEEDBACK Goal: Fast feedback to detect problems early. Improvement Kata (Very Important) 1. Vision 2. Current condition 3. Target condition 4. Small experiments Example: Pull request + peer review before merge. Third Way – Continual Learning & Experimentation Learn from failure and success.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782333391691-DevOps (1).pdf-page-2-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 13:36:37.668625	009ce9c081146f39c6c128584c02529ec38f94652ed7f09fd9674cdcc01da008	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782333391691-DevOps (1).pdf", "pageNumber": 2}
136	31	1	processed\\pages\\1782334646870-Togaf-1.png	Togaf Chapter 1: Introduction to TOGAF ### Chapter 1: Introduction to TOGAF 1. What is TOGAF ? TOGAF (The Open Group Architecture Framework) is an enterprise architecture framework. It helps organizations design, develop, use, and maintain enterprise architectures using best practices and an iterative method. --- 2. Purpose of TOGAF TOGAF provides a standard method to align IT systems with business goals. --- 3. Definition of Architecture (in TOGAF) Architecture means: 1. A formal description of a system. 2. The structure of components, their relationships, and the rules governing their evolution. --- 4. Types of Architecture in TOGAF (Important) TOGAF covers four types of architecture: - Business Architecture: business strategy, processes, and governance. - Data Architecture: structure and management of data. - Application Architecture: applications and their interactions. - Technology Architecture: hardware, software, and networks.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782334646870-Togaf.pdf-page-1-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 13:57:30.968412	bed27d4724b81d73015618d4cb1c0e1a3d47e26ca5717b5efd74091e0e83cb49	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782334646870-Togaf.pdf", "pageNumber": 1}
137	31	2	processed\\pages\\1782334646870-Togaf-2.png	--- 5. Main Components of TOGAF TOGAF contains: - Architecture Development Method (ADM) - ADM Guidelines and Techniques - Architecture Content Framework - Enterprise Continuum - TOGAF Reference Models - Architecture Capability Framework --- 6. Architecture Development Method (ADM) ADM is the core of TOGAF. It describes the phases used to develop enterprise architecture based on business requirements. --- 7. Enterprise Continuum It is a model used to organize and reuse architecture and solution artifacts. --- 8. Why TOGAF is Important - Uses best practices - Supports reuse - Aligns business and IT - Provides a clear development method	processed\\qrcodes\\1782334646870-Togaf.pdf-page-2-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-24 13:57:30.994449	b1bf6bc3206cfa75989d8055a178c08db18c03a69f01e5bd5a48213bd87ffc7d	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782334646870-Togaf.pdf", "pageNumber": 2}
