139	32	2	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-02.png	3 Origins and Evolution Kanban is a Japanese word that literally means " visual card ". Kanban cards were originally used in Toyota to limit the amount of inventory tied up in " work in progress " on a manufacturing floor. Kanban not only reduces excess inventory waste, but also the time spent in producing it. The original author of Kanban was Taiichi Ohno . Kanban has been used in Lean Production (Lean Thinking) KANBAN was formalized in 2010 by David Anderson	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-2-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:58:52.706455	b1d5f0098f68bb754ce7d5be4b3cb459ab4d96a41450b2f97de282d20a016a66	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 2}
140	32	3	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-03.png	Kanban Concepts	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-3-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:58:53.510735	646658ffe0608ceecceacabf55c0843957ebc60a28ab41659b6c99c5655e321a	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 3}
141	32	4	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-04.png	5 Kanban Concept Cores Visualize Visualize Workflow • Split the entire work into defined sections or states, visualized as named columns on a wall. • Write each item on a card and put in a column to indicate where the item is in the workflow. Limit Limit WIP • Assign explicit limits to how many items can be in progress at each workflow section / state. • i.e., Work in Progress (WIP) is limited in each workflow state. Measure Measure the Lead Time • Lead Time, also known as cycle time, is the average time to complete one item. • Measure the Lead Time and optimize the process to make the Lead Time as small and predictable as possible. Direct implementation of a Lean Pull Scheduling System . An item can move to the next segment / state only when it obtains a slot in there.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-4-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:58:54.336386	03fbedf8d2eed3a09dbe1ca2e533390940f218006bb830cbbb20c0f4955685f1	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 4}
142	32	5	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-05.png	6 Kanban Board In a KANBAN process, the practice of visualization consists in making visible the state of the project by using a table and cards customized This is can also be done in a virtual way using computer softwares	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-5-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:58:55.157052	2aa6d33e7595915887493d14fb9a0f5e93feb02386d8872896fac35e7116d459	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 5}
143	32	6	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-06.png	Kanban Benefits & Characteristics	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-6-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:58:55.968073	f637ca7aa7f9b2a643a264b8afb172c55559eead5ee4dfa213796b50d3e8580d	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 6}
144	32	7	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-07.png	8 Kanban Benefits • It clearly identifies the Bottlenecks in real-time. • It is useful for situations where operations and support teams have a high level of uncertainty and variability . • It increases visibility of everything that is going on at the company. – All segments/states in the workflow are visually organized , – Reducing the wait times and ensuring speed • Reduces inventory in the range of 25%-75%, thereby reducing company costs. • It allows to avoid inventory overproduction, thereby saving resources and time as well. – This is termed as eliminating waste . • In agile, if values are combined with Kanban characteristics, the outcome would be Agile Kanban . – Popular practice in software engineering	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-7-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:58:56.774349	2d944d1768efe920527652e360beebe4bea3381075d03e94f24b78a2d43940a6	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 7}
316	64	15	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-15.png	Kanban Practices	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-15-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:55.672505	312c4cb346a2de6a0d1d7268f76cbbe82a797744dd99991fb710af5d8497c7d7	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 15}
145	32	8	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-08.png	9 Kanban Characteristics Flexibility in Planning • Visual representation of the workflow allows creating clearly marked flow lanes and indicating where each item is in the workflow. • If a task needs longer duration, it is allowed to execute without limitation, and at the same time, the tasks that are completed will flow to the next state. – Sufficient duration for longer tasks that cannot be broken down logically. – Preservation of value of such longer tasks. – Effort required by each role to be expended. – Continuous flow of the tasks that are completed without wait time.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-8-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:58:57.592291	cf211cc5f7e3103d9ea59e85c91d8d7c340ae1aaad55dea7f1c9576a3918fe98	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 8}
146	32	9	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-09.png	10 Kanban Characteristics Limits Work-In-Progress (WIP) • Explicit limits of Work-In-Progress (WIP) are assigned to number of items that can be in progress at each workflow state, indicated by a column. • This allows: – Reducing wait time. – Avoiding stress on resources at a workflow state. – Identifying bottlenecks causing an item to be in a workflow state than the anticipated time (usually average cycle time) immediately. – Resolving bottlenecks with collaboration of the entire team. – Decreasing dependencies in completing a task by splitting it into sub-tasks, so that the sub-task is tracked independently.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-9-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:58:58.401934	b068be2baf0c8c065c986efb6a9d4146559ab719185e04f566005bda30e3d006	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 9}
147	32	10	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-10.png	11 Kanban Characteristics Pull Approach • The importance of this approach consists in resolving friction between teams. – i.e., two teams collaborate while the first one is performing better than the second one, – it is likely that it pushes more work than the other can handle. – In Pull Approach, the next team pulls work only when it is ready for it. • Pull Approach is implemented by adding a buffer with limited capacity between the two teams. • The benefits of Pull Approach are: – Avoids piling-up of work. – Reduces wait time. – Facilitates a team to maintain constant pace and focus on quality. – Provides resource balancing.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-10-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:58:59.208868	cfa228458f0d5cd2c3eda7d0a14e155881d9e45b457180f64ff0b45d6965dbf1	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 10}
148	32	11	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-11.png	12 Kanban Characteristics Minimize Cycle Time • The cycle time for each task is measured and the process is optimized to reduce the cycle times. • The bottlenecks are identified immediately and resolved collaboratively by the entire team. • The correction loops are considered to reduce rework.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-11-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:59:00.034916	116b235df997cea86c353436f09434fddaec7140e4409555479a80ab7caeb4cc	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 11}
149	32	12	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-12.png	13 Kanban Characteristics Visual Metrics • Visually organized workflows (on Kanban Boards) facilitate: – Scheduling as per WIP limits on a workflow state. – Tracking status and progress continually. – Assigning resources dynamically based on the role requirements.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-12-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:59:00.840961	ae80f6a59a1cb3663e67675a365174b59b61938d6f8a67b33d1963efc3539e50	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 12}
150	32	13	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-13.png	14 Kanban Characteristics Continuous Delivery Benefits of continuous delivery are: • Short release cycles result in continuous delivery of growing product at regular interval. • Continuous interactions with the customer. • To understand what customer wants. • Produce what the customer needs. • Feedback on delivered modules. • Limited requirements in each release cycle. • Developers are not overloaded with requests. This enables them to focus on the delivery. • There is no partially completed work. • Focus is on finishing work than on starting work. • This enables focus on sustaining pace and quality of the product. • Deliver before the customer changes mind. • Optimize flow of Work from beginning to end. • Helps in incremental process improvements.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-13-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:59:01.653992	b9a639f384b87ce1e604064c27855caef5c161c026b1aa8eebfcb5826831cbc0	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 13}
151	32	14	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-14.png	15 Kanban Characteristics Efficiency Through Focus The focus is on delivering value to the customer. • A customer's expectations can be made realistic and focused with continuous interactions with the customer. • Focus on the tasks is ensured with a limit on work-in-progress (WIP). • The Pull approach enables resources to complete the tasks at hand before a new task is taken up. • Optimizing lead-time (cycle time) results in faster delivery. • Visualization of the workflow with Kanban board draws immediate attention to any bottlenecks that can be resolved immediately. • Empowerment of the team makes the team accountable for the success. Efficiency can be achieved in the following ways:	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-14-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:59:02.472158	4f2495423ac99c141258183e47d29e635429d2a3a45a177a44c809a7acc5ae33	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 14}
152	32	15	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-15.png	Kanban Practices	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-15-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:59:03.297222	7d68e44c6ea658e2314b5bf4d70dc372bcc8aa521572d7c53d4d8e40a24b530e	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 15}
153	32	16	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-16.png	17 Kanban Practices A KANBAN process encompasses in general 6 types of practices: Visualization Limitation of Work in Progress (WIP) Control by measure Improvement Detailed explanation of rules Adopting PDSA model	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-16-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:59:04.110226	1ae62bab3ca73096fb26468825ccc1962f86d9470662ba6eb80264627909cbdd	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 16}
154	32	17	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-17.png	18 Vizualization In KANBAN process, the visualization practice consists in making visible the state of the project by using a board and customized cards Visualization can also be performed in a virtual way using computer software (ie., Jira)	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-17-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:59:04.924361	ddcd5903efe42932ab2c711a4795893461fb57cc8eb34cb53b15b157bdd93ec3	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 17}
155	32	18	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-18.png	19 WIP Limitation • WIP (Work In Progress) is streamlined thanks to a limitation of tasks and elements in progress for each activity of the process • Once established , this limit should not be exceeded by the team • The definition of the done must be well defined and understood in the same way by all the team members as well as by stakeholders	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-18-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:59:05.745012	5ff1cdb06fe5602fe37084cc5d5be76a060e4437f8b75861097c822ca2835faa	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 18}
156	32	19	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-19.png	20 Control and measure • The control of the elements movement is managed by indicators and measures allowing to anticipate the blockages cases and the possible prioritizations • The daily meetings address and organize the workflow • One example of measurement is that of the cycle time, it is the measuring of the duration (time) necessary to carry out a task or a element	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-19-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:59:06.554035	343f798b250f08def6503c1ee9b07fa736a62a2c0020616312e758330775425c	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 19}
157	32	20	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-20.png	21 Improvement The blockages and problems encountered are the trigger for a collaborative dialogue within the team in order to: • clearly specify the improvements to be made during an iteration , • and to determine the changes to be made.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-20-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:59:07.365849	f2aa80d2e1887fd2137dc801518eec86c824d05e8e82630b426aa64bec86bc02	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 20}
158	32	21	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-21.png	22 Kanban Rules Explanation • The KANBAN process rules must be explicit and visible to team members to: – ensure a shared understanding of the work , problems encountered and improvements future • One example of a rule consists in defining the common notion of the " Definition of Done " by all the team members .	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-21-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:59:08.175206	6a953103a1130c9d577f558b05c95c8411aa175ae54998841954469232f6eee5	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 21}
159	32	22	processed\\pages\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework-22.png	23 PDSA Model The KANBAN approach is taken from the model PDSA : The phase Plan (planning) consists of identifying a design model and defining the actions to be lead The phase Do (execute) represents the realization and implementation The phase Study (study) is the analysis and study phase The phase Act (acting) is the phase of adjusting and consolidating the model	processed\\qrcodes\\1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-22-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-25 12:59:08.990287	0025f303849409874f9ab9eef055c7ccc0e68ba07ff67f63a539109285c645fd	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782417499801-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 22}
