171	35	11	processed\\pages\\1782553576113-back-end+login-11.png	generate the JWT token The methods that will be used are generateToken(), isTokenValid() and getExpirationTime(). To generate the JWT token, we need a secret key and the token expiration time; these values are read from the application configuration properties file using the annotation @Value. We must update the application.properties to define these values:	processed\\qrcodes\\1782553576113-back-end+login.pdf-page-11-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-27 02:46:57.498631	567e50b40b6123457b3a6a5915947225ede7eb57a532409f8410e5e609d77141	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782553576113-back-end+login.pdf", "pageNumber": 11}
172	35	12	processed\\pages\\1782553576113-back-end+login-12.png	HMAC-SHA256 Hash Generator The secret key must be an HMAC hash string of 256 bits; otherwise, the token generation will throw an error. https://www.devglan.com/online-tools/hmac-sha256-online	processed\\qrcodes\\1782553576113-back-end+login.pdf-page-12-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-27 02:46:58.307639	0dacfea855ec5ae7ee69ed618e56935b370a73cb86c836aacc9c5942c6ee6f00	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782553576113-back-end+login.pdf", "pageNumber": 12}
173	35	13	processed\\pages\\1782553576113-back-end+login-13.png	Override the security configuration 1 - Perform the authentication by finding the user in our database. 2 - Generate a JWT token when the authentication succeeds. To override the implementation, let's create a package configs , add the file ApplicationConfiguration.java	processed\\qrcodes\\1782553576113-back-end+login.pdf-page-13-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-27 02:46:59.126021	4f00a515f22d657de2719e470ccc9ae809b236a7a211c2da1700d30010cc9a0a	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782553576113-back-end+login.pdf", "pageNumber": 13}
174	35	14	processed\\pages\\1782553576113-back-end+login-14.png	Create the authentication middleware For every request, we want to retrieve the JWT token in the header "Authorization", and validate it: 1 - If the token is invalid, reject the request if the token is invalid or continues otherwise. 2 - If the token is valid, extract the username, find the related user in the database, and set it in the authentication context so you can access it in any application layer. In the package configs , create a file JwtAuthenticationFilter.java	processed\\qrcodes\\1782553576113-back-end+login.pdf-page-14-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-27 02:46:59.930736	7183593fefb602fb77f9ec868b2f4325ad0122cc6dd1042acf4751bdff4e1a9f	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782553576113-back-end+login.pdf", "pageNumber": 14}
175	35	15	processed\\pages\\1782553576113-back-end+login-15.png	Configure the application requester filter 1. There is no need to provide the CSRF token because we will use it. 2. The request URL path matching /auth/signup and /auth/login doesn't require authentication. 3. Any other request URL path must be authenticated. 4. The request is stateless, meaning every request must be treated as a new one, even if it comes from the same client or has been received earlier. 5. Must use the custom authentication provider, and they must be executed before the authentication middleware. 6. The CORS configuration must allow only POST and GET requests. In the package configs , create a file SecurityConfiguration.java	processed\\qrcodes\\1782553576113-back-end+login.pdf-page-15-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-27 02:47:00.742739	7d8dd3890fc2e3055455079d13499557f209e2307e2b5e16fbcbf6952d637f58	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782553576113-back-end+login.pdf", "pageNumber": 15}
