211	42	2	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-02.png	3 Origins and Evolution Kanban is a Japanese word that literally means " visual card ". Kanban cards were originally used in Toyota to limit the amount of inventory tied up in " work in progress " on a manufacturing floor. Kanban not only reduces excess inventory waste, but also the time spent in producing it. The original author of Kanban was Taiichi Ohno . Kanban has been used in Lean Production (Lean Thinking) KANBAN was formalized in 2010 by David Anderson	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-2-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:05.880105	3873807f53d69d885ee6d5c76242f7bf2355e8e84d9e5db2f42de3ea83d320e5	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 2}
212	42	3	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-03.png	Kanban Concepts	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-3-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:06.695791	12e30cd68a15b084a4a41221b90dbe06d66c82c0177979c45cfede26147575a1	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 3}
213	42	4	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-04.png	5 Kanban Concept Cores Visualize Visualize Workflow • Split the entire work into defined sections or states, visualized as named columns on a wall. • Write each item on a card and put in a column to indicate where the item is in the workflow. Limit Limit WIP • Assign explicit limits to how many items can be in progress at each workflow section / state. • i.e., Work in Progress (WIP) is limited in each workflow state. Measure Measure the Lead Time • Lead Time, also known as cycle time, is the average time to complete one item. • Measure the Lead Time and optimize the process to make the Lead Time as small and predictable as possible. Direct implementation of a Lean Pull Scheduling System . An item can move to the next segment / state only when it obtains a slot in there.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-4-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:07.503873	897a469d04301a071c238f686d4979e8912171debc36527088f687499c4b0898	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 4}
214	42	5	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-05.png	6 Kanban Board In a KANBAN process, the practice of visualization consists in making visible the state of the project by using a table and cards customized This is can also be done in a virtual way using computer softwares	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-5-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:08.315365	732de64da1ef46a8ecd3171554469cd068778eb0035e68cc014194450a04027c	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 5}
215	42	6	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-06.png	Kanban Benefits & Characteristics	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-6-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:09.121557	ca109cb0f1c3f260b3b0b50c0d084e27b34dc4d63ac903992cc6bf13416bb595	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 6}
216	42	7	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-07.png	8 Kanban Benefits • It clearly identifies the Bottlenecks in real-time. • It is useful for situations where operations and support teams have a high level of uncertainty and variability . • It increases visibility of everything that is going on at the company. – All segments/states in the workflow are visually organized , – Reducing the wait times and ensuring speed • Reduces inventory in the range of 25%-75%, thereby reducing company costs. • It allows to avoid inventory overproduction, thereby saving resources and time as well. – This is termed as eliminating waste . • In agile, if values are combined with Kanban characteristics, the outcome would be Agile Kanban . – Popular practice in software engineering	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-7-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:09.941438	28c741be4f1abfa0d94a5e6b497b6ccb71e8c946ff245f4a11efb695072d8b90	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 7}
217	42	8	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-08.png	9 Kanban Characteristics Flexibility in Planning • Visual representation of the workflow allows creating clearly marked flow lanes and indicating where each item is in the workflow. • If a task needs longer duration, it is allowed to execute without limitation, and at the same time, the tasks that are completed will flow to the next state. – Sufficient duration for longer tasks that cannot be broken down logically. – Preservation of value of such longer tasks. – Effort required by each role to be expended. – Continuous flow of the tasks that are completed without wait time.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-8-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:10.773762	48ee08939dfbfb92f91fd5d6530964690c228fcb602ca0d6d83481647b540906	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 8}
218	42	9	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-09.png	10 Kanban Characteristics Limits Work-In-Progress (WIP) • Explicit limits of Work-In-Progress (WIP) are assigned to number of items that can be in progress at each workflow state, indicated by a column. • This allows: – Reducing wait time. – Avoiding stress on resources at a workflow state. – Identifying bottlenecks causing an item to be in a workflow state than the anticipated time (usually average cycle time) immediately. – Resolving bottlenecks with collaboration of the entire team. – Decreasing dependencies in completing a task by splitting it into sub-tasks, so that the sub-task is tracked independently.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-9-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:11.585907	9254ed580fdf658a8602f6721f22990150d0605dbb7da9404fc8f786bff0e5ed	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 9}
219	42	10	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-10.png	11 Kanban Characteristics Pull Approach • The importance of this approach consists in resolving friction between teams. – i.e., two teams collaborate while the first one is performing better than the second one, – it is likely that it pushes more work than the other can handle. – In Pull Approach, the next team pulls work only when it is ready for it. • Pull Approach is implemented by adding a buffer with limited capacity between the two teams. • The benefits of Pull Approach are: – Avoids piling-up of work. – Reduces wait time. – Facilitates a team to maintain constant pace and focus on quality. – Provides resource balancing.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-10-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:12.390774	067c4cf691120c6dd6d98cf329eaee7807fc869999070f60505599c64ccca448	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 10}
220	42	11	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-11.png	12 Kanban Characteristics Minimize Cycle Time • The cycle time for each task is measured and the process is optimized to reduce the cycle times. • The bottlenecks are identified immediately and resolved collaboratively by the entire team. • The correction loops are considered to reduce rework.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-11-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:13.207208	76b96dbe2d476b297bd679c26e0da169c67aa7df9416f5c86284d9b98e6e59dc	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 11}
221	42	12	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-12.png	13 Kanban Characteristics Visual Metrics • Visually organized workflows (on Kanban Boards) facilitate: – Scheduling as per WIP limits on a workflow state. – Tracking status and progress continually. – Assigning resources dynamically based on the role requirements.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-12-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:14.02383	6138bc503b2ad3c19e4e484a4d1ec7524e7e977fdbd16d082c30222f9a365602	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 12}
222	42	13	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-13.png	14 Kanban Characteristics Continuous Delivery Benefits of continuous delivery are: • Short release cycles result in continuous delivery of growing product at regular interval. • Continuous interactions with the customer. • To understand what customer wants. • Produce what the customer needs. • Feedback on delivered modules. • Limited requirements in each release cycle. • Developers are not overloaded with requests. This enables them to focus on the delivery. • There is no partially completed work. • Focus is on finishing work than on starting work. • This enables focus on sustaining pace and quality of the product. • Deliver before the customer changes mind. • Optimize flow of Work from beginning to end. • Helps in incremental process improvements.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-13-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:14.835745	e5fba62021d65eb47e7ae838548585836ec10ff01027d134a820cd64c0011e22	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 13}
223	42	14	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-14.png	15 Kanban Characteristics Efficiency Through Focus The focus is on delivering value to the customer. • A customer's expectations can be made realistic and focused with continuous interactions with the customer. • Focus on the tasks is ensured with a limit on work-in-progress (WIP). • The Pull approach enables resources to complete the tasks at hand before a new task is taken up. • Optimizing lead-time (cycle time) results in faster delivery. • Visualization of the workflow with Kanban board draws immediate attention to any bottlenecks that can be resolved immediately. • Empowerment of the team makes the team accountable for the success. Efficiency can be achieved in the following ways:	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-14-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:15.668302	826db2fb34b51b8bd9ac2747637a8a375bb4875a356f19a39522e238fd5115f4	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 14}
225	42	16	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-16.png	17 Kanban Practices A KANBAN process encompasses in general 6 types of practices: Visualization Limitation of Work in Progress (WIP) Control by measure Improvement Detailed explanation of rules Adopting PDSA model	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-16-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:17.299115	60832355380bb4de086b890478bb7cbff3c81f3e0783b16d37f57f8129e64f7b	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 16}
226	42	17	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-17.png	18 Vizualization In KANBAN process, the visualization practice consists in making visible the state of the project by using a board and customized cards Visualization can also be performed in a virtual way using computer software (ie., Jira)	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-17-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:18.122675	a01d6181a310df071555de4f85d7c950c47cedca19cf142c4bc3e1b97f3d6331	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 17}
227	42	18	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-18.png	19 WIP Limitation • WIP (Work In Progress) is streamlined thanks to a limitation of tasks and elements in progress for each activity of the process • Once established , this limit should not be exceeded by the team • The definition of the done must be well defined and understood in the same way by all the team members as well as by stakeholders	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-18-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:18.951957	1f5bf11a5f96cc39b0b7d36aeb973a1948e299fe684c1024436cd3f445fed384	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 18}
228	42	19	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-19.png	20 Control and measure • The control of the elements movement is managed by indicators and measures allowing to anticipate the blockages cases and the possible prioritizations • The daily meetings address and organize the workflow • One example of measurement is that of the cycle time, it is the measuring of the duration (time) necessary to carry out a task or a element	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-19-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:19.774869	f699c498bf786a80be2e209ae30f71491cbfeae5fd3a29cf073fa23ce7752193	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 19}
229	42	20	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-20.png	21 Improvement The blockages and problems encountered are the trigger for a collaborative dialogue within the team in order to: • clearly specify the improvements to be made during an iteration , • and to determine the changes to be made.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-20-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:20.604339	177c588faa66f3a03a12f200ae3ab2ac24d4f566ed2b793740932cc175e1234c	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 20}
230	42	21	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-21.png	22 Kanban Rules Explanation • The KANBAN process rules must be explicit and visible to team members to: – ensure a shared understanding of the work , problems encountered and improvements future • One example of a rule consists in defining the common notion of the " Definition of Done " by all the team members .	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-21-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:21.414864	9a22698d37493cafe87b4ce20dcb9270bb9ff55644f74552dfeef606ab631896	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 21}
231	42	22	processed\\pages\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework-22.png	23 PDSA Model The KANBAN approach is taken from the model PDSA : The phase Plan (planning) consists of identifying a design model and defining the actions to be lead The phase Do (execute) represents the realization and implementation The phase Study (study) is the analysis and study phase The phase Act (acting) is the phase of adjusting and consolidating the model	processed\\qrcodes\\1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-22-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 04:19:22.227888	6b8e0d18f1882e5274a1926f6728ddea1268cbdb6b9c791e7686b116dbacb805	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782731911783-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 22}
