255	47	2	processed\\pages\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2-02.png	Abstract Modern database systems must efficiently process large volumes of data while maintaining fast query response times. One of the most critical techniques used to improve performance is indexing. However, traditional indexing strategies are static, meaning that a predefined index is used regardless of the query type. With the rapid growth of data, database systems must efficiently process large volumes of queries. Indexing plays a crucial role in improving query performance, but selecting the appropriate indexes is a complex and dynamic problem. This project focuses on adaptive index selection techniques for optimizing query execution in large-scale databases. The proposed approach analyzes query workloads and dynamically recommends or adjusts indexes to enhance performance while minimizing storage and maintenance costs. A prototype system was developed using PostgreSQL to evaluate different indexing strategies, including B-Tree and Hash indexes. Experimental results demonstrate significant improvements in query execution time when adaptive indexing strategies are applied. This work highlights the importance of intelligent, workload-aware indexing in modern database systems and provides insights into automated optimization techniques. The outcome shows that no one kind of indexes can give the best results under any workload conditions. The B-Tree index had good performance, Hash indexes proved to be useful in equality searching, and the Bitmap-like approach was most successful in low cardinality cases involving multiple conditions. The use of adaptive approach led to a significant gain in performance; in some cases, the execution time was reduced by more than 99%.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx-page-2-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 06:02:13.364185	db6482b166b152c0d4fb702df73c36ca555c9baebc25a31def90c0bf25f4d3a3	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx", "pageNumber": 2}
256	47	3	processed\\pages\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2-03.png	Chapter 1: Introduction 1.1 Context Modern applications generate massive amounts of data, requiring efficient database systems to store and retrieve information. Query performance is a critical factor in system efficiency. 1.2 Problem Statement Choosing the right indexes manually is difficult due to changing query patterns and large data volumes. Poor index selection can lead to slow performance. Traditional indexing strategies use static index selection and do not adapt to query type. Static indexing is a fixed set of indexes defined at design time. It cannot adapt to changing query patterns or data distribution. But queries vary, we have: 1- Equality queries 2- Range queries 3- Aggregation operations PS: Some queries may not use any index → full table scans. This leads to inefficient execution, increased response time, and resource waste. 1.3 Objectives  To evaluate the performance of different index types  To compare B-Tree, Hash, and Bitmap indexing techniques  To analyze query performance across different query patterns  To design and implement an adaptive index selection system  To improve query performance dynamically 1.4 Methodology The methodology includes workload analysis, index creation, performance measurement, and adaptive optimization.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx-page-3-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 06:02:14.17695	6bc61f3f6568e251dff7aea19a7187d7a85d2184ea368c719b1328577465cb2c	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx", "pageNumber": 3}
257	47	4	processed\\pages\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2-04.png	Chapter 2: Background & Literature Review 2.1 Database Indexing Indexes are data structures that improve data retrieval speed. Common types include B- Tree and Hash indexes. A database index is a separate data structure, maintained alongside the main table, that stores a subset of the table's data (typically one or more column values) in a form that supports rapid lookups. An index works similarly to the index at the back of a textbook: instead of reading every page to find a topic, you consult the index to jump directly to the relevant page. In database terms, the index maps column values to the physical locations (page and row offset) of matching records, enabling the query engine to retrieve data without scanning irrelevant rows. 2.2 Query Optimization Query optimizers choose execution plans based on cost estimation. Indexes reduce query execution time. The performance gap between indexed and non-indexed queries grows dramatically with table size. For a table with N rows, a sequential scan requires O(N) page reads. A well-designed B-Tree index reduces this to O(log N). Consider a table with 500,000 rows: a sequential scan may read thousands of disk pages, while an index scan might read only 3 or 4 index levels before locating the target row. In practice, however, the relationship between indexing and performance is not straightforward. Indexes introduce overhead on write operations (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE), consume additional storage space, and must be carefully chosen to match the actual query workload. This is why adaptive index selection, the central theme of this project, is a critical area of database optimization research. 2.3 Related Work Research explores automated indexing, adaptive systems, and self-tuning databases.	processed\\qrcodes\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx-page-4-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 06:02:14.996744	78c20d67014793c21afba1d177f92ff1c16125377992156e4f81420faf67caa4	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx", "pageNumber": 4}
