261	47	8	processed\\pages\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2-08.png	5.2.3 Baseline Analysis Results: A ll queries used parallel sequential scan, resulting in high execution times. PostgreSQL is reading every single row in the table from start to finish, but using multiple CPU cores at the same time to do it faster. 5.2.4 B-Tree Analysis Index Creation: CREATE INDEX idx_btree_customer ON sales(customer_id); CREATE INDEX idx_btree_product ON sales(product_id); CREATE INDEX idx_btree_date ON sales(sale_date); CREATE INDEX idx_btree_amount ON sales(amount); CREATE INDEX idx_btree_category ON sales(category); Results: B-Tree significantly improved performance due to its ordered structure. 5.2.5 Hash Analysis Q# Query Description SQL Pattern Scan Type (from EXPLAIN) Planning Time (ms) Execution Time (ms) Buffer Q1 Equality WHERE customer_id = 1234; Parallel Seq Scan on sales 0.195 123.925 4821 Q2 Range WHERE sale_date BETWEEN '2023-01-01' AND '2023-02- 01'; Parallel Seq Scan on sales 0.15 118.563 4821 Q3 Aggregation + GROUP BY SELECT category, AVG(amount) GROUP BY category; Parallel Seq Scan on sales (Gather Merge+Sort+Partial HashAggregate) 0.253 247.901 4837 Q4 Multi-condition WHERE category = 'Electronics' AND region = 'North'; Parallel Seq Scan on sales 0.072 141.358 4821 PHASE 1 — Baseline Queries (No Indexes / Sequential Scan) Run these queries BEFORE creating any index. Record EXPLAIN ANALYZE output below. Q# Query Description SQL Pattern Scan Type (from EXPLAIN) Planning Time (ms) Execution Time (ms) Buffer Q1 Equality WHERE customer_id = 1234;' Bitmap Index Scan on idx_btree_customer 3.741 0.232 shared hit=54 read=3 Q2 Range WHERE sale_date BETWEEN '2022-01-01' AND '2022-06- 01'; Bitmap Index Scan on idx_btree_date 0.156 6.635 shared hit=4017 read=11 Q3 Aggregation + GROUP BY SELECT category, AVG(amount) FROM sales GROUP BY category; Parallel Seq Scan on sales (Gather Merge+Sort+Partial HashAggregate) 0.207 241.331 shared hit=4837 Q4 Multi-condition WHERE category = 'Electronics' AND region = 'North'; Bitmap Index Scan on idx_category 0.607 24.829 shared hit=4821 read=56 PHASE 2 — B-TREE TEST Q# Query Description SQL Pattern Scan Type (from EXPLAIN) Planning Time (ms) Execution Time (ms) Buffer Q1 Equality WHERE customer_id = 1234;' Bitmap Index Scan on idx_hash_customer 3.949 0.559 shared hit=57 Q2 Range WHERE sale_date BETWEEN '2022-01-01' AND '2022-06- 01'; Parallel Seq Scan on sales 0.181 102.310 shared hit=4821 Q3 Aggregation + GROUP BY SELECT category, AVG(amount) FROM sales GROUP BY category; Parallel Seq Scan on sales (Gather Merge+Sort+Partial HashAggregate) 0.147 223.027 shared hit=4837 Q4 Multi-condition WHERE category = 'Electronics' AND region = 'North'; Bitmap Index Scan on idx_hash_category 0.182 31.964 shared hit=4975 PHASE 3 — HASH INDEX TEST	processed\\qrcodes\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx-page-8-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 06:02:18.294313	47f5f0930575585e91bf46e3eb623efc2f1de52df0f96ebc5442811b73f19c16	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx", "pageNumber": 8}
262	47	9	processed\\pages\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2-09.png	Index Dropping of B-Tree: DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_btree_customer; DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_btree_product; DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_btree_date; DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_btree_amount; DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_btree_category; Index Creation of Hash: CREATE INDEX idx_hash_customer ON sales USING hash(customer_id); CREATE INDEX idx_hash_product ON sales USING hash(product_id); CREATE INDEX idx_hash_date ON sales USING hash(sale_date); CREATE INDEX idx_hash_category ON sales USING hash(category); Results: Hash performed well for equality but failed for range queries. 5.2.6 Bitmap Analysis Index Dropping of Hash: DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_hash_customer; DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_hash_product; DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_hash_date; DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_hash_category; Index Creation of Bitmap: CREATE INDEX idx_brin_category ON sales USING brin(category); CREATE INDEX idx_brin_region ON sales USING brin(region); CREATE INDEX idx_brin_status ON sales USING brin(status); Results:  Best for low-cardinality filtering  Efficient for combined conditions Q# Query Description SQL Pattern Scan Type (from EXPLAIN) Planning Time (ms) Execution Time (ms) Buffer Q1 Equality WHERE customer_id = 1234;' Bitmap Index Scan on idx_id 1.585 0.531 shared hit=54 read=3 Q2 Range WHERE sale_date BETWEEN '2022-01-01' AND '2022-06- 01'; Parallel Seq Scan on sales 0.18 172.543 shared hit=4821 Q3 Aggregation + GROUP BY SELECT category, AVG(amount) FROM sales GROUP BY category; Parallel Seq Scan on sales (Gather Merge+Sort+Partial HashAggregate) 0.179 250.379 shared hit=4837 Q4 Multi-condition WHERE category = 'Electronics' AND region = 'North'; Bitmap Index Scan on idx_category 0.174 24.686 shared hit=4877 PHASE 4 — LOW CARDINALITY (BITMAP BEHAVIOR)	processed\\qrcodes\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx-page-9-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 06:02:19.104434	f9779ddad3b645287865e8e6035034fb4f68975f4cffdf5d5f9b53aa4b952a89	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx", "pageNumber": 9}
263	47	10	processed\\pages\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2-10.png	5.2.6 Final Comparison Final Conclusion  Hash index is Best for equality and Ignored for range  B-Tree Works for both equality and range and Most versatile  Bitmap/BRIN is best for low-cardinality filters and Efficient for multi-condition queries  PostgreSQL optimizer: Automatically avoids bad index usage In PostgreSQL, Hash indexes are almost never chosen over B-Tree indexes in practice. B- Tree handles equality just as fast AND supports range queries, sorting, and Index Only Scans, making Hash indexes largely redundant. So we conclude that B-Tree won because it's more versatile:  B-Tree Index Only Scan → read index, and it will be done  Hash Index Scan → read index, then fetch table rows ( slower) We also observe GROUP BY which means PostgreSQL must read every single row to calculate AVG for all groups, so no index can help skip anything. Indexes are ignored whenever the query needs to touch most or all of the table. Aggregations, GROUP BY on low-cardinality columns, and high-match Boolean filters all fall into this category. Range queries, aggregations, and ORDER BY will still use sequential scan or B-Tree. Also we observe that:  Q ueries that need to merge results from two separate index lookups → Bitmap OR  Q ueries that need to intersect results from two different columns → Bitmap AND Query / Scenario Baseline Execution time (ms) B-Tree Execution time (ms) Hash Execution time (ms) Bitmap Execution time (ms) Best Index Improvement vs Baseline (%) Q1 — Equality 123.925 0.232 0.559 0.531 B-Tree 99.81% Q2 — Range 118.563 6.635 102.310 172.543 B-Tree 94.40% Q3 — Aggregation + GROUP BY 247.901 241.331 223.027 250.379 Hash 10.03% Q4 — Multi-condition 141.358 24.829 31.964 24.686 Bitmap 82.54% FINAL COMPARISON — All Index Types vs Baseline	processed\\qrcodes\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx-page-10-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 06:02:19.914028	1f87329f6d6c12cf98400c8539cab5749ee196e0e1b70d4d8c6ebd9ef7cf86e8	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx", "pageNumber": 10}
264	47	11	processed\\pages\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2-11.png	Chapter 6: Adaptive Index Selection Algorithm System An adaptive system was implemented to dynamically select the best index. Steps: 1. Classify query (Equality / Range / Aggregation) 2. Analyze column statistics (cardinality, selectivity) 3. Estimate cost using models 4. Apply feasibility rules 5. Select index with lowest cost Cost Models:  B-Tree Equality → log₂(n)  B-Tree Range → log₂(n) + selectivity × n  Hash → constant time (O (1))  Bitmap → n / 64  Full Scan → n Implementation An adaptive selector was implemented in Python using:  psycopg2 for database connectivity  Query parsing for classification  PostgreSQL statistics ( pg_stats )  Dynamic index creation and execution  The system:  Analyzes queries  Selects optimal index  Executes queries  Measures performance Discussion The results confirm that:  No single index type is optimal for all queries  B-Tree is the most versatile index  Hash is highly efficient but limited  Bitmap is effective for low-cardinality conditions  Additionally, PostgreSQL sometimes avoids indexes due to cost-based optimization, demonstrating that index usage depends on both query type and data distribution. Limitations  Rule-based system (not machine learning)  Single-column indexing focus  Bitmap index simulated (not native in PostgreSQL)  Simplified query parsing Code	processed\\qrcodes\\1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx-page-11-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-06-29 06:02:20.721914	7fed02543b81d96b8b84b90eeb3a210b104bcad4fe7b7a13f7fb288b4f8da740	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1782738092048-adaptive_index_report2.docx", "pageNumber": 11}
