303	64	2	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-02.png	3 Origins and Evolution Kanban is a Japanese word that literally means " visual card ". Kanban cards were originally used in Toyota to limit the amount of inventory tied up in " work in progress " on a manufacturing floor. Kanban not only reduces excess inventory waste, but also the time spent in producing it. The original author of Kanban was Taiichi Ohno . Kanban has been used in Lean Production (Lean Thinking) KANBAN was formalized in 2010 by David Anderson	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-2-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:45.023748	8a4c8b774a27cf27073fc9dc47bf1a2a17e1c37c235316e4b1ab47b53b92f89e	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 2}
304	64	3	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-03.png	Kanban Concepts	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-3-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:45.844221	bd522c450690684aa344fe3a0c535b7861899edaceb1d9e213ec0d97814e6832	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 3}
305	64	4	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-04.png	5 Kanban Concept Cores Visualize Visualize Workflow • Split the entire work into defined sections or states, visualized as named columns on a wall. • Write each item on a card and put in a column to indicate where the item is in the workflow. Limit Limit WIP • Assign explicit limits to how many items can be in progress at each workflow section / state. • i.e., Work in Progress (WIP) is limited in each workflow state. Measure Measure the Lead Time • Lead Time, also known as cycle time, is the average time to complete one item. • Measure the Lead Time and optimize the process to make the Lead Time as small and predictable as possible. Direct implementation of a Lean Pull Scheduling System . An item can move to the next segment / state only when it obtains a slot in there.	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-4-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:46.665236	f425cb947575a3a919d1963271b31ee372867e5cf772ec56d439dbac24aba7bd	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 4}
308	64	7	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-07.png	8 Kanban Benefits • It clearly identifies the Bottlenecks in real-time. • It is useful for situations where operations and support teams have a high level of uncertainty and variability . • It increases visibility of everything that is going on at the company. – All segments/states in the workflow are visually organized , – Reducing the wait times and ensuring speed • Reduces inventory in the range of 25%-75%, thereby reducing company costs. • It allows to avoid inventory overproduction, thereby saving resources and time as well. – This is termed as eliminating waste . • In agile, if values are combined with Kanban characteristics, the outcome would be Agile Kanban . – Popular practice in software engineering	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-7-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:49.115563	15f42109cc3462f07621fc38fb2ea21102c9581e613b096357f67a7de2a63dad	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 7}
309	64	8	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-08.png	9 Kanban Characteristics Flexibility in Planning • Visual representation of the workflow allows creating clearly marked flow lanes and indicating where each item is in the workflow. • If a task needs longer duration, it is allowed to execute without limitation, and at the same time, the tasks that are completed will flow to the next state. – Sufficient duration for longer tasks that cannot be broken down logically. – Preservation of value of such longer tasks. – Effort required by each role to be expended. – Continuous flow of the tasks that are completed without wait time.	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-8-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:49.936663	277a2fad3be88efa21d0f245312c58d99dc7b7d86d75984019885a7fa37866ea	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 8}
310	64	9	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-09.png	10 Kanban Characteristics Limits Work-In-Progress (WIP) • Explicit limits of Work-In-Progress (WIP) are assigned to number of items that can be in progress at each workflow state, indicated by a column. • This allows: – Reducing wait time. – Avoiding stress on resources at a workflow state. – Identifying bottlenecks causing an item to be in a workflow state than the anticipated time (usually average cycle time) immediately. – Resolving bottlenecks with collaboration of the entire team. – Decreasing dependencies in completing a task by splitting it into sub-tasks, so that the sub-task is tracked independently.	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-9-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:50.763574	b56027fc119f2789bf18007ffb702232ffbe6775f7ed33999a4b4b48716b1258	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 9}
311	64	10	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-10.png	11 Kanban Characteristics Pull Approach • The importance of this approach consists in resolving friction between teams. – i.e., two teams collaborate while the first one is performing better than the second one, – it is likely that it pushes more work than the other can handle. – In Pull Approach, the next team pulls work only when it is ready for it. • Pull Approach is implemented by adding a buffer with limited capacity between the two teams. • The benefits of Pull Approach are: – Avoids piling-up of work. – Reduces wait time. – Facilitates a team to maintain constant pace and focus on quality. – Provides resource balancing.	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-10-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:51.5771	b063ddc1849232d092471f7e2a44e427cdfd14d070bf052990b37a8ca6d2bc00	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 10}
312	64	11	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-11.png	12 Kanban Characteristics Minimize Cycle Time • The cycle time for each task is measured and the process is optimized to reduce the cycle times. • The bottlenecks are identified immediately and resolved collaboratively by the entire team. • The correction loops are considered to reduce rework.	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-11-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:52.392352	dd6d9958d0957ae811baaff072fc607596573633ae96a7b3579f41597061ef25	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 11}
313	64	12	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-12.png	13 Kanban Characteristics Visual Metrics • Visually organized workflows (on Kanban Boards) facilitate: – Scheduling as per WIP limits on a workflow state. – Tracking status and progress continually. – Assigning resources dynamically based on the role requirements.	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-12-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:53.218654	ecc73661c860711e530f7dec73d1c69e420b4a43b0bb39f5da0829e54ac84129	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 12}
314	64	13	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-13.png	14 Kanban Characteristics Continuous Delivery Benefits of continuous delivery are: • Short release cycles result in continuous delivery of growing product at regular interval. • Continuous interactions with the customer. • To understand what customer wants. • Produce what the customer needs. • Feedback on delivered modules. • Limited requirements in each release cycle. • Developers are not overloaded with requests. This enables them to focus on the delivery. • There is no partially completed work. • Focus is on finishing work than on starting work. • This enables focus on sustaining pace and quality of the product. • Deliver before the customer changes mind. • Optimize flow of Work from beginning to end. • Helps in incremental process improvements.	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-13-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:54.030979	8bd0b596efacb1ff76a0c4f817da9cff479c4523080d1c5106fd4fce537a8dbb	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 13}
315	64	14	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-14.png	15 Kanban Characteristics Efficiency Through Focus The focus is on delivering value to the customer. • A customer's expectations can be made realistic and focused with continuous interactions with the customer. • Focus on the tasks is ensured with a limit on work-in-progress (WIP). • The Pull approach enables resources to complete the tasks at hand before a new task is taken up. • Optimizing lead-time (cycle time) results in faster delivery. • Visualization of the workflow with Kanban board draws immediate attention to any bottlenecks that can be resolved immediately. • Empowerment of the team makes the team accountable for the success. Efficiency can be achieved in the following ways:	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-14-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:54.850928	085d98c67bb05ab50aa3ef9a48ddbe48ff3b4b9ec652b567d9e7423f4f591083	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 14}
318	64	17	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-17.png	18 Vizualization In KANBAN process, the visualization practice consists in making visible the state of the project by using a board and customized cards Visualization can also be performed in a virtual way using computer software (ie., Jira)	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-17-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:57.299163	0d6fe34ad95102f998799682520a200bca3849a22b6c948514f411ea285596b9	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 17}
319	64	18	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-18.png	19 WIP Limitation • WIP (Work In Progress) is streamlined thanks to a limitation of tasks and elements in progress for each activity of the process • Once established , this limit should not be exceeded by the team • The definition of the done must be well defined and understood in the same way by all the team members as well as by stakeholders	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-18-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:58.12333	e9d8dda353e2c1fd2e6ca16f0186cbe6364872f5bd144e77bf8e53a487f39fab	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 18}
320	64	19	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-19.png	20 Control and measure • The control of the elements movement is managed by indicators and measures allowing to anticipate the blockages cases and the possible prioritizations • The daily meetings address and organize the workflow • One example of measurement is that of the cycle time, it is the measuring of the duration (time) necessary to carry out a task or a element	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-19-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:58.94069	5f59b7c0d7f4dc8bf78f3f76ccfccdf6ac044aecc44c38c4d5e61ef4e18e905c	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 19}
321	64	20	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-20.png	21 Improvement The blockages and problems encountered are the trigger for a collaborative dialogue within the team in order to: • clearly specify the improvements to be made during an iteration , • and to determine the changes to be made.	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-20-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:38:59.756998	86c2201390f33cca83cb65d952a38da58ba8553c2c4276f6eb820b4fbd7f76dc	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 20}
322	64	21	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-21.png	22 Kanban Rules Explanation • The KANBAN process rules must be explicit and visible to team members to: – ensure a shared understanding of the work , problems encountered and improvements future • One example of a rule consists in defining the common notion of the " Definition of Done " by all the team members .	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-21-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:39:00.56852	11518436f6a7eb0b37cbbf44ca2ba8e022c05bde57af9adcb0064fc78740739c	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 21}
323	64	22	processed\\pages\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework-22.png	23 PDSA Model The KANBAN approach is taken from the model PDSA : The phase Plan (planning) consists of identifying a design model and defining the actions to be lead The phase Do (execute) represents the realization and implementation The phase Study (study) is the analysis and study phase The phase Act (acting) is the phase of adjusting and consolidating the model	processed\\qrcodes\\1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf-page-22-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-07-06 03:39:01.389197	bc4831c6ee920d2459b8f7a54d016e7e3cfb92bc4d5546587cadf4824f5dae37	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1783334293369-Kanban Framework.pdf", "pageNumber": 22}
