325	78	1	processed\\pages\\1785941279244-GraphQL-1.png	GraphQL is a query language for APIs and a runtime for executing those queries against request .your data. It was developed by Facebook in 2012 and open-sourced in 2015. Unlike REST, which relies on multiple endpoints for different resources, GraphQL allows clients to request exactly the data they need in a single Endpoint. 🔹 Key Features of GraphQL: 1. Flexible Queries – Clients specify the structure of the response, reducing over- fetching and under-fetching of data. 2. Single Endpoint – Unlike REST APIs, which require multiple endpoints, GraphQL operates via a single endpoint (/graphql). 3. Strongly Typed Schema – The API is defined using a GraphQL schema , specifying data types and relationships. 4. Real-Time Updates – Supports subscriptions for real-time data updates. 5. Efficient and Faster – Reduces unnecessary network requests and payload size. 🔹 Example: 📌 GraphQL Query: query { user(id: 1) { name email posts { title content } } }	processed\\qrcodes\\1785941279244-GraphQL.docx-page-1-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-08-05 07:48:25.017184	03acc30803030d174ab06bd203cc4a687c2925e707de8e5d88e0808c2424640f	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1785941279244-GraphQL.docx", "pageNumber": 1}
326	78	2	processed\\pages\\1785941279244-GraphQL-2.png	📌 Response: { "data": { "user": { "name": "John Doe", "email": "john@example.com", "posts": [ { "title": "GraphQL Basics", "content": "GraphQL is awesome!" } ] } } } Here, the client only requests the name, email, and posts of the user, without unnecessary data. 🔹 GraphQL vs REST: Feature GraphQL REST Data Fetching Fetches only needed data Over-fetches/Under-fetches Endpoints Single endpoint (/graphql) Multiple endpoints Real-time Supports subscriptions Requires WebSockets Versioning No versioning needed Requires versioning (v1, v2) Schema Strongly typed schema Depends on implementation Here's a step-by-step guide to setting up GraphQL in Eclipse with Java Spring Boot :	processed\\qrcodes\\1785941279244-GraphQL.docx-page-2-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-08-05 07:48:25.866926	2d7a9bb702516e0a3581f0bb64ae64609f8a856a3dd72bc520c825169dd1aa51	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1785941279244-GraphQL.docx", "pageNumber": 2}
327	78	3	processed\\pages\\1785941279244-GraphQL-3.png	Step 1: Install Eclipse and Setup a Spring Boot Project 1. Download and install Eclipse IDE (if not installed) from: https://www.eclipse.org/downloads/ 2. Install Spring Tools (if not already installed): o Go to Help → Eclipse Marketplace → Search for Spring Tools 4 and install it. 3. Create a Spring Boot Project : o Open Eclipse → File → New → Spring Starter Project o Enter project name (e.g., graphql-demo) o Choose Spring Boot version : 3.x or 2.x o Add dependencies:  Spring Web  Spring Boot DevTools  GraphQL for Spring Boot  Lombok (optional, for reducing boilerplate code) Step 2: Add Dependencies in pom.xml Modify pom.xml to include GraphQL support: <dependencies> <!-- Spring Boot Starter Web --> <dependency> <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId> <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId> </dependency> <!-- Spring Boot Starter GraphQL --> <dependency> <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId> <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-graphql</artifactId> </dependency>	processed\\qrcodes\\1785941279244-GraphQL.docx-page-3-qr.png	f	native-pdf	\N	\N	2026-08-05 07:48:26.68307	be02cecd7054b2ddea03d60a2de538ccd49dcf8372bf79918e856770d294b312	{"v": 1, "type": "page", "checksum": "demo-checksum", "documentId": "1785941279244-GraphQL.docx", "pageNumber": 3}
