224	256	chapter, introduction, context, modern, applications, generate, massive, amounts, data,, requiring, efficient, database, systems, store, retrieve, information., query, performance, critical, factor, system, efficiency., problem, statement, choosing, right, indexes, manually, difficult, changing, query, patterns, large, data, volumes., poor, index, selection, lead, slow, performance., traditional, indexing, strategies, static, index, selection, adapt, query, type.	Chapter 1: Introduction 1.1 Context Modern applications generate massive amounts of data, requiring efficient database systems to store and retrieve information. Query performance is a critical factor in system efficiency. 1.2 Problem Statement Choosing the right indexes manually is difficult due to changing query patterns and large data volumes. Poor index selection can lead to slow performance. Traditional indexing strategies use static index selection and do not adapt to query type. Static indexing is a fixed set of indexes defined at design time. It cannot adapt to changing query patterns or data distribution. But queries vary, we have: 1- Equality queries 2- Range queries 3- Aggregation operations PS: Some queries may not use any index → full table scans. This leads to inefficient execution, increased response time, and resource waste. 1.3 Objectives  To evaluate the performance of different index types  To compare B-Tree, Hash, and Bitmap indexing techniques  To analyze query performance across different query patterns  To design and implement an adaptive index selection system  To improve query performance dynamically 1.4 Methodology The methodology includes workload analysis, index creation, performance measurement, and adaptive optimization.	xenova-minilm-224	2026-06-29 06:02:26.518277	[0.006593339145183563, 0.045506808906793594, 0.012474928982555866, 0.08828175812959671, -0.04456246271729469, -0.04483763501048088, -0.002599175088107586, 0.009394592605531216, -0.044844888150691986, -0.