225	257	chapter, background, literature, review, database, indexing, indexes, data, structures, that, improve, data, retrieval, speed., common, types, include, tree, hash, indexes., database, index, separate, data, structure,, maintained, alongside, main, table,, that, stores, subset, table's, data, (typically, more, column, values), form, that, supports, rapid, lookups., index, works, similarly, index, back, textbook:, instead	Chapter 2: Background & Literature Review 2.1 Database Indexing Indexes are data structures that improve data retrieval speed. Common types include B- Tree and Hash indexes. A database index is a separate data structure, maintained alongside the main table, that stores a subset of the table's data (typically one or more column values) in a form that supports rapid lookups. An index works similarly to the index at the back of a textbook: instead of reading every page to find a topic, you consult the index to jump directly to the relevant page. In database terms, the index maps column values to the physical locations (page and row offset) of matching records, enabling the query engine to retrieve data without scanning irrelevant rows. 2.2 Query Optimization Query optimizers choose execution plans based on cost estimation. Indexes reduce query execution time. The performance gap between indexed and non-indexed queries grows dramatically with table size. For a table with N rows, a sequential scan requires O(N) page reads. A well-designed B-Tree index reduces this to O(log N). Consider a table with 500,000 rows: a sequential scan may read thousands of disk pages, while an index scan might read only 3 or 4 index levels before locating the target row. In practice, however, the relationship between indexing and performance is not straightforward. Indexes introduce overhead on write operations (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE), consume additional storage space, and must be carefully chosen to match the actual query workload. This is why adaptive index selection, the central theme of this project, is a critical area of database optimization research. 2.3 Related Work Research explores automated indexing, adaptive systems, and self-tuning databases.	xenova-minilm-225	2026-06-29 06:02:26.52174	[0.004353267140686512, 0.021313602104783058, -0.03165514022111893, 0.06072215363383293, 0.013334370218217373, -0.03141644597053528, 0.0015818050596863031, 0.0033941122237592936, 0.030836593359708786, 0.04112886264920235, -0.061530888080596924, 0.06917738914489746, 0.055286601185798645, -0.039615992456674576, -0.06418242305517197, -0.02352205291390419, 0.032395653426647186, 0.03968598693609238, -0.05331355333328247, 0.005915688816457987, 0.05117008462548256, -0.013293590396642685, 0.040515877306461334, -0.01005968451499939, -0.07982584834098816, 0.011221681721508503, 0.0003936839057132602, 0.011350675486028194, -0.024777855724096298, 0.008737597614526749, -0.05373820662498474, 0.10193409025669098, 0.015462896786630154, 0.07879337668418884, -0.13217128813266754, -0.026201674714684486, 0.03157573565840721, 0.008681527338922024, 0.023033007979393005, -0.02151837758719921, -0.0705987736582756, 0.029592696577310562, -0.06871536374092102, 0.02069188840687275, 0.06783828139305115, 0.030061498284339905, -0.024627450853586197, -0.019421687349677086, -0.0635153129696846, -0.005934541113674641, -0.15977561473846436, -0.00015024658932816237, -0.04512195661664009, -0.00213392311707139, 0.0006694571929983795, 0.05995865911245346, -0.007146791554987431, 0.0006253394531086087, 0.014467904344201088, 0.0343051552772522, 0.01337859034538269, -0.015980202704668045, 0.033190660178661346, -0.04475090652704239, 0.047153208404779434, -0.055718667805194855, 0.08510538190603256, -0.058598898351192474, 0.06281158328056335, -0.05038216710090637, 0.004600624553859234, 0.08930298686027527, -0.07272632420063019, 0.015937982127070427, -0.0361628420650959, -0.022614212706685066, -0.007344652898609638, 0.03517576679587364, 0.012693287804722786, -0.0258882325142622, -0.