232	264	chapter, adaptive, index, selection, algorithm, system, adaptive, system, implemented, dynamically, select, best, index., steps:, classify, query, (equality, range, aggregation), analyze, column, statistics, (cardinality,, selectivity), estimate, cost, using, models, apply, feasibility, rules, select, index, with, lowest, cost, cost, models:, b-tree, equality, log₂(n), b-tree, range, log₂(n), selectivity, hash, constant, time, (1)), bitmap	Chapter 6: Adaptive Index Selection Algorithm System An adaptive system was implemented to dynamically select the best index. Steps: 1. Classify query (Equality / Range / Aggregation) 2. Analyze column statistics (cardinality, selectivity) 3. Estimate cost using models 4. Apply feasibility rules 5. Select index with lowest cost Cost Models:  B-Tree Equality → log₂(n)  B-Tree Range → log₂(n) + selectivity × n  Hash → constant time (O (1))  Bitmap → n / 64  Full Scan → n Implementation An adaptive selector was implemented in Python using:  psycopg2 for database connectivity  Query parsing for classification  PostgreSQL statistics ( pg_stats )  Dynamic index creation and execution  The system:  Analyzes queries  Selects optimal index  Executes queries  Measures performance Discussion The results confirm that:  No single index type is optimal for all queries  B-Tree is the most versatile index  Hash is highly efficient but limited  Bitmap is effective for low-cardinality conditions  Additionally, PostgreSQL sometimes avoids indexes due to cost-based optimization, demonstrating that index usage depends on both query type and data distribution. Limitations  Rule-based system (not machine learning)  Single-column indexing focus  Bitmap index simulated (not native in PostgreSQL)  Simplified query parsing Code	xenova-minilm-232	2026-06-29 06:02:26.539899	[-0.017761223018169403, -0.01686166785657406, -0.0007165698334574699, 0.07990774512290955, -0.025855692103505135, -0.07120261341333389, -0.019194472581148148