223	255	abstract, modern, database, systems, must, efficiently, process, large, volumes, data, while, maintaining, fast, query, response, times., most, critical, techniques, used, improve, performance, indexing., however,, traditional, indexing, strategies, static,, meaning, that, predefined, index, used, regardless, query, type., with, rapid, growth, data,, database, systems, must, efficiently, process, large, volumes, queries., indexing, plays	Abstract Modern database systems must efficiently process large volumes of data while maintaining fast query response times. One of the most critical techniques used to improve performance is indexing. However, traditional indexing strategies are static, meaning that a predefined index is used regardless of the query type. With the rapid growth of data, database systems must efficiently process large volumes of queries. Indexing plays a crucial role in improving query performance, but selecting the appropriate indexes is a complex and dynamic problem. This project focuses on adaptive index selection techniques for optimizing query execution in large-scale databases. The proposed approach analyzes query workloads and dynamically recommends or adjusts indexes to enhance performance while minimizing storage and maintenance costs. A prototype system was developed using PostgreSQL to evaluate different indexing strategies, including B-Tree and Hash indexes. Experimental results demonstrate significant improvements in query execution time when adaptive indexing strategies are applied. This work highlights the importance of intelligent, workload-aware indexing in modern database systems and provides insights into automated optimization techniques. The outcome shows that no one kind of indexes can give the best results under any workload conditions. The B-Tree index had good performance, Hash indexes proved to be useful in equality searching, and the Bitmap-like approach was most successful in low cardinality cases involving multiple conditions. The use of adaptive approach led to a significant gain in performance; in some cases, the execution time was reduced by more than 99%.	xenova-minilm-223	2026-06-29 06:02:26.515097	[-0.00681061577051878, 0.06296303123235703, 0.015310893766582012, 0.10049864649772644, -0.07683301717042923, -0.04848965257406235, -0.00378024741075933, 0.0186957735568285, -0.013931747525930405, 0.023473430424928665, -0.06769676506519318, 0.03661571070551872, 0.046401601284742355, -0.0431143194437027, -0.06100316718220711, -0.004832728765904903, 0.059953898191452026, 0.03856409341096878, -0.05939989164471626, -0.02300127036869526, -0.030601661652326584, -0.020924193784594536, -0.00905211828649044, 0.016422322019934654, -0.053313616663217545, -0.00966623518615961, 0.03566849231719971, -0.00833389163017273, 0.004384862259030342, -0.015960995107889175, -0.05894618481397629, 0.04765290394425392, 0.025107506662607193, 0.06862759590148926, -0.1315053254365921, -0.007067213300615549, 0.014604034833610058, -0.026833809912204742, 0.004155228845775127, -0.06821952760219574, -0.05677443742752075, 0.01418457180261612, -0.1114705502986908, 0.03310512751340866, -0.007186618167907, 0.05717454105615616, -0.0036087268963456154, 0.041628722101449966, 0.006964239291846752, 0.026037823408842087, -0.1106468141078949, -0.012394224293529987, -0.037109680473804474, -0.004269729368388653, 0.005246315151453018, 0.0630820244550705, -0.006619957275688648, -0.035543814301490784, 0.002868224401026964, 0.0010976319899782538, 0.020381871610879898, -0.019906060770154, 0.06719943881034851, -0.030066797509789467, 0.014148378744721413, -0.008267226628959179, 0.11812504380941391, -0.04501601681113243, 0.07194875180721283, -0.05842839181423187, -0.0002497571113053709, 0.09167839586734772, -0.15326668322086334, 0.06484391540288925, -0.10210732370615005, 0.04015877842903137, 0.0037509433459490538, -0.0016964580863714218, -0.03365199267864227, 0.021885264664888382, -0.05898567661643028, -0.004140717908740044, -0.00