239	271	chapter, final, conclusion, expected,, there, best, design, indexes., there, specific, cases, where, each, three, index, methods, will, perform, better, than, others., thus,, important, choose, most, appropriate, method, depending, particular, scenario., b-tree, indexes, have, shown, themselves, most, flexible, versatile, improve, performance, database, management, system., they, best, choice, equality, queries,, ranges,, sorting	Chapter 7: Final Conclusion As expected, there is no best way to design indexes. There are specific use cases where each of the three index methods will perform better than the others. Thus, it is important to choose the most appropriate method depending on the particular scenario. B-Tree indexes have shown themselves to be the most flexible and versatile way to improve the performance of a database management system. They are the best choice for equality queries, ranges, and sorting tasks. Bitmap indexes are more convenient for querying columns with low cardinality and applying filters with multiple conditions. Hashing can be effectively used for equality operations on high-cardinality fields. During the PostgreSQL tests, it turned out that the optimizer did not use the existing indexes in case the sequential scan was more optimal. This finding shows that creating indexes does not always lead to improved database efficiency. The developed system based on Python algorithms was able to analyze queries and select the optimal indexing approach. While there was additional overhead involved compared to PostgreSQL's built-in optimizer, this project successfully illustrated the concept of external intelligent index automation. To conclude, the findings demonstrate that using adaptive indexes in response to the type of query, number of distinct values of the columns in question, and the selectivity ratio could drastically increase query efficiency in some instances by as much as 99 percent without adding any superfluous indexes to the system.	xenova-minilm-239	2026-06-29 06:02:26.559421	[0.000877189333550632, 0.04168986901640892, 0.004444565158337355, 0.05995165556669235, -0.033841006457805634, -0.057840149849653244, 0.012433881871402264, 0.017035290598869324, -0.003600629046559334, 0.01711234450340271, -0.07120013236999512, 0.04329822212457657, 0.06679695844650269, -0.04285193234682083, -0.049675751477479935, 0.024348922073841095, 0.062196336686611176, -0.005182546563446522, -0.03929716348648071, -0.050677258521318436, -0.04333650320768356, -0.01961350627243519, -0.004053854383528233, 0.003442090703174472, -0.08510056138038635, -0.013038799166679382, -0.014233318157494068, 0.0005234595737420022, -0.0028566615656018257, -0.026626789942383766, -0.08057074248790741, 0.09042314440011978, 0.06183447316288948, 0.03539688512682915, -0.14133459329605103, -0.07273024320602417, 0.051639750599861145, -0.031119268387556076, -0.038062576204538345, -0.06433969736099243, -0.04686892405152321, 0.029362760484218597, -0.08507934957742691, 0.012983428314328194, -0.030534652993083, 0.06049971282482147, -0.020558582618832588, 0.01834241673350334, -0.04342593625187874, -0.018284639343619347, -0.12618915736675262, 0.05039422959089279, -0.07337480783462524, 0.01818455383181572, 0.008325938135385513, 0.024085668846964836, -0.06258609145879745, -0.05533993989229202, 0.035861287266016006, 0.025148415938019753, -0.02609773725271225, 0.017627187073230743, 0.047307029366493225, -0.02910361997783184, 0.03006361797451973, -0.01520503405481577, 0.10968180745840073, -0.0474986657500267, 0.09387765824794769, -0.016465436667203903, 0.03886013478040695, 0.07036829739809036, -0.12801650166511536, 0.03769969567656517, -0.056561607867479324, 0.04416542872786522, -0.00416844617575407, -0.011432214640080929, -0.022132888436317444, -0.012960702180862427, -0.059178903698921204, -0.030181193724274635, 0.017198672518134117, 0.025984687730669975, -0.009280294179916382, -0.15945543348789215, -0.027506142854690552, -0.034794941544532776, 0.03675762