231	263	5.2.6, final, comparison, final, conclusion, hash, index, best, equality, ignored, range, b-tree, works, both, equality, range, most, versatile, bitmap/brin, best, low-cardinality, filters, efficient, multi-condition, queries, postgresql, optimizer:, automatically, avoids, index, usage, postgresql,, hash, indexes, almost, never, chosen, over, b-tree, indexes, practice., tree, handles, equality, just, fast, supports, range, queries,, sorting,	5.2.6 Final Comparison Final Conclusion  Hash index is Best for equality and Ignored for range  B-Tree Works for both equality and range and Most versatile  Bitmap/BRIN is best for low-cardinality filters and Efficient for multi-condition queries  PostgreSQL optimizer: Automatically avoids bad index usage In PostgreSQL, Hash indexes are almost never chosen over B-Tree indexes in practice. B- Tree handles equality just as fast AND supports range queries, sorting, and Index Only Scans, making Hash indexes largely redundant. So we conclude that B-Tree won because it's more versatile:  B-Tree Index Only Scan → read index, and it will be done  Hash Index Scan → read index, then fetch table rows ( slower) We also observe GROUP BY which means PostgreSQL must read every single row to calculate AVG for all groups, so no index can help skip anything. Indexes are ignored whenever the query needs to touch most or all of the table. Aggregations, GROUP BY on low-cardinality columns, and high-match Boolean filters all fall into this category. Range queries, aggregations, and ORDER BY will still use sequential scan or B-Tree. Also we observe that:  Q ueries that need to merge results from two separate index lookups → Bitmap OR  Q ueries that need to intersect results from two different columns → Bitmap AND Query / Scenario Baseline Execution time (ms) B-Tree Execution time (ms) Hash Execution time (ms) Bitmap Execution time (ms) Best Index Improvement vs Baseline (%) Q1 — Equality 123.925 0.232 0.559 0.531 B-Tree 99.81% Q2 — Range 118.563 6.635 102.310 172.543 B-Tree 94.40% Q3 — Aggregation + GROUP BY 247.901 241.331 223.027 250.379 Hash 10.03% Q4 — Multi-condition 141.358 24.829 31.964 24.686 Bitmap 82.54% FINAL COMPARISON — All Index Types vs Baseline	xenova-minilm-231	2026-06-29 06:02:26.537054	[-0.0037048477679491043, 0.05408420041203499, 0.009849374182522297, 0.02092636004090309, -0.0038173699285835028, -0.010506776161491871, -0.027053581550717354, -0.002242659218609333, 0.024554260075092316, 0.029944663867354393, -0.08803846687078476, -0.02083679474890232, 0.025086380541324615, -0.045661721378564835, -0.03396051749587059, 0.06661782413721085, 0.05759923532605171, 0.018538473173975945, -0.03476138785481453, -0.05461656302213669, -0.012208551168441772, -0.028019102290272713, -0.01016115490347147, -0.03959641978144646, -0.029868463054299355, 0.033728376030921936, -0.045072853565216064, 0.02772601507604122, -0.011599446646869183, -0.06361006200313568, -0.07179668545722961, 0.09094888716936111, 0.04298149794340134, 0.030401987954974174, -0.15683802962303162, -0.027599811553955078, 0.05459413677453995, -0.038752228021621704, -0.011079947464168072, -0.055910512804985046, -0.06087612733244896, 0.09047769755125046, -0.0586710087954998, -0.010466951876878738, 0.019405053928494453, 0.05282367765903473, -0.025385122746229172, 0.027087366208434105, -0.04704153910279274, -0.05361749231815338, -0.07346250861883163, 0.04989178106188774, -0.04541198909282684, 0.06097804009914398, 0.015313771553337574, 0.044388964772224426, -0.0916786789894104, -0.0729060247540474, 0.024408914148807526, -0.002528101671487093, -0.04919155687093735, 0.017535636201500893, 0.0019047257956117392, -0.025395004078745842, 0.054707761853933334, -0.011772437952458858, 0.09427493810653687, -0.020847788080573082, 0.07377713173627853, 0.0041418541222810745, 0.012278118170797825, 0.03148435056209564, -0.1383417248725891, 0.03753891959786415, -0.04837093874812126, 0.05956631526350975, 0.03253965452313423, -0.009793479926884174, -0.0